1
00:00:26,570 --> 00:00:34,036
TOHO CO., LTD.

2
00:00:37,164 --> 00:00:41,168
A TOHO
20TH ANNIVERSARY FILM

3
00:00:43,128 --> 00:00:44,630
A 1952 ARTS FESTIVAL
SELECTION

4
00:00:47,507 --> 00:00:53,680
IKIRU
(To Live)

5
00:00:57,309 --> 00:01:00,354
Produced by
SOJIRO MOTOKI

6
00:01:02,147 --> 00:01:05,108
Screenplay by AKIRA KUROSAWA
SHINOBU HASHIMOTO, HIDEO OGUNI

7
00:01:07,027 --> 00:01:08,820
Cinematography by
ASAKAZU NAKAI

8
00:01:08,946 --> 00:01:10,864
Art Director
TAKASHI MATSUYAMA

9
00:01:12,908 --> 00:01:15,160
Music by
FUMIO HAYASAKA

10
00:01:17,079 --> 00:01:19,873
Edited by
KOICHI IWASHITA

11
00:01:22,876 --> 00:01:25,462
Starring

12
00:01:27,089 --> 00:01:29,633
TAKASHI SHIMURA

13
00:01:31,260 --> 00:01:35,305
SHINICHI HIMORI, HARUO TANAKA
MINORU CHIAKI, MIKI ODAGIRI

14
00:01:35,472 --> 00:01:37,933
BOKUZEN HIDARI, MINOSUKE
YAMADA, KAMATARI FUJIWARA

15
00:01:39,434 --> 00:01:41,436
MAKOTO KOBORI
NOBUO KANEKO

16
00:01:41,603 --> 00:01:43,563
NOBUO NAKAMURA
ATSUSHI WATANABE

17
00:01:43,730 --> 00:01:46,149
ISAO KIMURA, MASAO SHIMIZU
YUNOSUKE ITO

18
00:01:47,943 --> 00:01:51,446
KUMEKO URABE, EIKO MIYOSHI
NORIKO HOMMA, YATSUKO TANAMI

19
00:01:51,613 --> 00:01:54,408
KIN SUGAI, YOSHIE MINAMI
KYOKO SEKI

20
00:01:56,618 --> 00:01:59,621
KUSUO ABE
TOMOO NAGAI

21
00:01:59,788 --> 00:02:02,749
SEIJI MIYAGUCHI
DAISUKE KATO

22
00:02:19,099 --> 00:02:24,438
Directed by
AKIRA KUROSAWA

23
00:02:31,153 --> 00:02:35,407
This stomach belongs
to the protagonist of our story.

24
00:02:36,658 --> 00:02:39,494
Signs of cancer
are already present,

25
00:02:39,661 --> 00:02:42,456
but he doesn't know that yet.

26
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,961
PUBLIC AFFAIRS
SECTION CHIEF

27
00:03:11,818 --> 00:03:13,862
The water gives
my child rashes.

28
00:03:14,029 --> 00:03:16,198
WHERE CITIZENS
AND CITY HALL MEET

29
00:03:16,365 --> 00:03:19,368
PLEASE DON'T HESITATE TO SUBMIT
And it breeds mosquitoes.

30
00:03:19,534 --> 00:03:21,703
YOUR COMPLAINTS
AND REQUESTS HERE

31
00:03:21,870 --> 00:03:23,497
Can't you do something?

32
00:03:23,622 --> 00:03:27,417
It would make a great playground
if you filled it in.

33
00:03:28,627 --> 00:03:30,420
Just a moment, please.

34
00:03:37,844 --> 00:03:41,390
Sir, they've come to complain
about open sewage.

35
00:03:41,556 --> 00:03:43,725
Send them to Public Works.

36
00:03:48,897 --> 00:03:52,109
Here's our protagonist.

37
00:03:52,901 --> 00:03:57,030
But what a bore it would be
to describe his life now.

38
00:03:57,447 --> 00:03:59,074
Why?

39
00:03:59,324 --> 00:04:02,327
Because
he's only killing time.

40
00:04:02,744 --> 00:04:05,247
He's never actually lived.

41
00:04:05,414 --> 00:04:08,375
You can't call this living.

42
00:04:18,093 --> 00:04:19,845
Miss Odagiri...

43
00:04:20,011 --> 00:04:22,431
how dare you,
during business hours?

44
00:04:22,556 --> 00:04:24,099
It's just so funny!

45
00:04:24,266 --> 00:04:25,934
What is?

46
00:04:26,101 --> 00:04:29,020
The “Liar's Club.”
Someone sent it around.

47
00:04:30,021 --> 00:04:31,898
Read it to us.

48
00:04:37,446 --> 00:04:40,490
“So you've never taken
a vacation?”

49
00:04:40,615 --> 00:04:41,867
“That's right.”

50
00:04:41,992 --> 00:04:44,578
“Because City Hall
can't run without you?”

51
00:04:44,744 --> 00:04:47,122
“No, because everyone
would realize

52
00:04:47,289 --> 00:04:50,625
City Hall doesn't
need me at all.”

53
00:05:03,597 --> 00:05:06,725
This isn't even worth watching.

54
00:05:07,058 --> 00:05:09,644
He might as well be a corpse.

55
00:05:09,978 --> 00:05:14,858
In fact, he's been dead
for some 20 years now.

56
00:05:16,026 --> 00:05:18,820
Before that,
he had some life in him.

57
00:05:19,112 --> 00:05:22,365
He even tried
to do a little real work.

58
00:05:27,329 --> 00:05:30,624
PROPOSAL FOR INCREASING
DEPARTMENTAL EFFICIENCY

59
00:05:30,749 --> 00:05:33,835
SUBMITTED 11/7/1930
BY KANJI WATANABE

60
00:05:36,546 --> 00:05:40,759
But there's nothing left
of that will or passion.

61
00:05:41,301 --> 00:05:44,554
They've been completely
worn down

62
00:05:44,721 --> 00:05:48,183
by the minutia
of the bureaucratic machine

63
00:05:48,308 --> 00:05:51,228
and the meaningless
busyness it breeds.

64
00:05:56,900 --> 00:05:59,402
Busy... so terribly busy.

65
00:06:00,111 --> 00:06:04,157
But in reality,
he does nothing at all

66
00:06:04,449 --> 00:06:07,577
besides protecting
his own position.

67
00:06:08,078 --> 00:06:11,373
The best way to protect
your place in this world

68
00:06:11,540 --> 00:06:13,708
is to do nothing at all.

69
00:06:16,211 --> 00:06:18,588
But is this enough?

70
00:06:24,553 --> 00:06:26,763
Is this really enough?

71
00:06:28,098 --> 00:06:31,393
For him to start
thinking seriously about this,

72
00:06:31,560 --> 00:06:34,729
his stomach
will have to get a lot worse,

73
00:06:35,063 --> 00:06:40,026
and he'll have to rack up
a lot more wasted time.

74
00:06:40,944 --> 00:06:44,364
But any proposal
for creating a park

75
00:06:44,573 --> 00:06:46,741
must go
to the Parks Department.

76
00:06:47,325 --> 00:06:50,787
This seems to be more
a matter of sanitation.

77
00:06:50,954 --> 00:06:53,498
Talk to your
district health center.

78
00:06:53,999 --> 00:06:55,959
See the Sanitation Department.

79
00:06:56,459 --> 00:06:57,919
See Environmental Sanitation.

80
00:06:59,004 --> 00:07:00,755
Preventive Sanitation.

81
00:07:01,089 --> 00:07:02,757
Disease Control.

82
00:07:03,300 --> 00:07:05,260
Lots of mosquitoes?

83
00:07:05,510 --> 00:07:08,263
That's a job for Pest Control.

84
00:07:12,142 --> 00:07:16,062
The problem is seeping waste,
which means...

85
00:07:16,855 --> 00:07:20,609
it's a problem for
the Sewage Department at City Hall.

86
00:07:21,443 --> 00:07:24,446
That was originally a culvert.

87
00:07:24,571 --> 00:07:27,449
That is,
it had a road over it.

88
00:07:27,616 --> 00:07:29,618
You'll need the okay
of the Roads Department.

89
00:07:30,410 --> 00:07:34,205
City Planning doesn't have
a policy for such cases.

90
00:07:34,748 --> 00:07:36,124
Go to Zoning.

91
00:07:37,042 --> 00:07:42,047
The Fire Department objected
to filling that cesspool.

92
00:07:42,964 --> 00:07:45,634
They need it because
water pressure is bad there.

93
00:07:46,092 --> 00:07:47,636
Are you kidding?

94
00:07:47,761 --> 00:07:50,055
What we need is water,

95
00:07:50,221 --> 00:07:54,267
not sewage that breeds mosquitoes
and causes rashes.

96
00:07:54,643 --> 00:07:58,647
Imagine cleaning that sewage
out of our hoses!

97
00:07:58,897 --> 00:08:03,943
If there were a kiddie pool there,
that'd be great for us too.

98
00:08:04,569 --> 00:08:07,322
What about
your local school district?

99
00:08:07,530 --> 00:08:10,992
They should have
a Child Welfare section.

100
00:08:11,868 --> 00:08:16,122
But this problem
doesn't only affect children.

101
00:08:16,331 --> 00:08:20,710
Rebuilding all the schools
has exhausted our resources.

102
00:08:20,877 --> 00:08:23,254
A problem this big
should be handled

103
00:08:23,421 --> 00:08:26,508
by your city councilman.

104
00:08:27,050 --> 00:08:30,887
Sure, I'll write an introduction
to the deputy mayor.

105
00:08:31,054 --> 00:08:34,724
Take my business card.
He'll see you right away.

106
00:08:37,060 --> 00:08:39,062
Please have a seat.

107
00:08:39,229 --> 00:08:42,190
Thank you
for all your efforts.

108
00:08:43,108 --> 00:08:46,069
There's really nothing
we welcome more

109
00:08:46,486 --> 00:08:50,281
than folks like you
who bring grievances like this

110
00:08:50,865 --> 00:08:53,535
directly to our attention.

111
00:08:54,244 --> 00:08:56,871
It's precisely
for people like you

112
00:08:56,996 --> 00:09:00,041
that we established
our new Public Affairs section.

113
00:09:00,500 --> 00:09:02,961
Don't hesitate
to tell them all about it!

114
00:09:03,128 --> 00:09:06,131
Show these nice folks
to the Public Affairs desk.

115
00:09:07,465 --> 00:09:11,428
You'll need to talk
to Public Works. Desk 8.

116
00:09:22,147 --> 00:09:25,650
How dare you!
Stop giving us the runaround!

117
00:09:25,817 --> 00:09:28,069
Why did you even put up
that sign?

118
00:09:28,194 --> 00:09:30,071
So we'd waste our time?

119
00:09:30,238 --> 00:09:33,116
We're not idlers
like all of you!

120
00:09:33,283 --> 00:09:37,662
We just want something done
about that stinking cesspool!

121
00:09:37,912 --> 00:09:40,582
We don't care
if it's Public Works, Sanitation,

122
00:09:40,749 --> 00:09:43,126
Public Health, or whoever!

123
00:09:43,293 --> 00:09:45,378
That's for Public Affairs
to sort out!

124
00:09:45,545 --> 00:09:48,131
Fine!
We're done with you jerks!

125
00:09:48,298 --> 00:09:50,341
All you do is laugh at us!

126
00:09:50,467 --> 00:09:52,260
You call this democracy?

127
00:09:52,427 --> 00:09:53,928
Let's go.

128
00:10:08,735 --> 00:10:10,445
Please wait a moment.

129
00:10:11,321 --> 00:10:14,783
I'm afraid our section chief
is out today.

130
00:10:14,949 --> 00:10:18,578
It would be best
if you could put it in writing.

131
00:10:29,589 --> 00:10:31,800
PUBLIC AFFAIRS
SECTION CHIEF

132
00:10:35,595 --> 00:10:37,472
What do you think?

133
00:10:37,639 --> 00:10:40,558
The section chief
never takes time off.

134
00:10:40,725 --> 00:10:44,938
Yeah. And he's been
listless recently.

135
00:10:45,980 --> 00:10:49,984
But what'll we do
if he's out for a long time?

136
00:10:50,276 --> 00:10:51,861
Exactly.

137
00:10:52,153 --> 00:10:55,740
He wouldn't take a day off
just for a cold.

138
00:10:56,282 --> 00:10:58,618
And nothing moves here
without his seal.

139
00:10:58,743 --> 00:10:59,953
That's right.

140
00:11:01,454 --> 00:11:05,500
I must say,
it's a terrible shame.

141
00:11:06,084 --> 00:11:08,002
One more month

142
00:11:08,169 --> 00:11:10,255
and he'd have set a record

143
00:11:10,421 --> 00:11:12,966
of 30 years
without a single absence.

144
00:11:13,675 --> 00:11:16,469
Some people are glad
he isn't here.

145
00:11:16,803 --> 00:11:19,973
Bureaucrats always wonder
who'll take the boss's place

146
00:11:20,098 --> 00:11:22,183
if he steps out.

147
00:11:25,270 --> 00:11:27,939
He's been taking a lot of medicine.
What for?

148
00:11:28,106 --> 00:11:29,607
His stomach.

149
00:11:29,732 --> 00:11:32,694
He used to slurp
his noodles to the bottom,

150
00:11:32,861 --> 00:11:35,864
but lately
he barely touches them.

151
00:11:36,072 --> 00:11:39,117
Plain udon noodles in soup.
Yet another record.

152
00:11:39,242 --> 00:11:42,078
I've never seen him eat
anything else.

153
00:11:43,162 --> 00:11:46,624
Assuming the worst,
who'll replace him?

154
00:11:49,043 --> 00:11:50,879
What's your rush?

155
00:11:51,004 --> 00:11:55,425
A lot of guys have to die
before it's your turn!

156
00:12:03,016 --> 00:12:05,435
X-RAY ROOM

157
00:12:20,450 --> 00:12:22,994
- Mr. Hiraoka!
- Yes.

158
00:12:59,906 --> 00:13:01,699
Your stomach?

159
00:13:04,202 --> 00:13:06,913
My stomach's bad too.

160
00:13:07,413 --> 00:13:09,415
They say it's chronic.

161
00:13:10,959 --> 00:13:15,129
These days I hardly feel alive
unless my stomach hurts.

162
00:13:17,465 --> 00:13:20,093
Mr. Ko Suzuki.

163
00:13:24,097 --> 00:13:26,224
That man who just went in -

164
00:13:30,645 --> 00:13:33,481
His doctor told him
he has an ulcer,

165
00:13:34,232 --> 00:13:37,860
but I'm sure
it's stomach cancer.

166
00:13:38,444 --> 00:13:40,363
Has to be.

167
00:13:42,240 --> 00:13:46,327
And stomach cancer
is practically a death sentence.

168
00:13:46,995 --> 00:13:50,456
The doc usually says
it's just a mild ulcer

169
00:13:50,581 --> 00:13:53,835
and that there's
no need to operate.

170
00:13:54,961 --> 00:13:58,673
That you can eat
whatever you want

171
00:13:58,798 --> 00:14:01,092
as long as
it's easy to digest.

172
00:14:01,342 --> 00:14:05,471
If that's what he tells you,
you've got a year at most.

173
00:14:06,014 --> 00:14:11,144
But with some symptoms,
you won't even last a year:

174
00:14:12,395 --> 00:14:13,980
first...

175
00:14:15,481 --> 00:14:17,692
a dull, heavy pain.

176
00:14:18,609 --> 00:14:21,738
Frequent unpleasant burping.

177
00:14:23,698 --> 00:14:25,700
A dry tongue.

178
00:14:26,284 --> 00:14:29,078
You can't seem to get
enough water and tea.

179
00:14:29,704 --> 00:14:32,457
And then
there's the diarrhea.

180
00:14:33,291 --> 00:14:37,045
And if it isn't diarrhea,
it's constipation.

181
00:14:38,254 --> 00:14:40,757
Your bowel movements
turn black.

182
00:14:44,552 --> 00:14:46,304
And then...

183
00:14:46,888 --> 00:14:51,267
the meat you used to love
seems totally inedible.

184
00:14:52,727 --> 00:14:56,439
Whatever you eat
you throw up half an hour later.

185
00:14:56,606 --> 00:15:00,234
And then you start
throwing up food

186
00:15:00,401 --> 00:15:02,695
you ate a week before.

187
00:15:02,987 --> 00:15:06,115
At that point, you've got
three months at most...

188
00:15:17,794 --> 00:15:21,339
Mr. Kanji Watanabe.

189
00:15:23,925 --> 00:15:26,094
Mr. Kanji Watanabe.

190
00:15:28,930 --> 00:15:30,431
Yes.

191
00:16:08,094 --> 00:16:09,637
Have a seat.

192
00:16:12,557 --> 00:16:16,477
Now, then... it looks like
you have a mild ulcer.

193
00:16:31,492 --> 00:16:33,452
Please...

194
00:16:33,703 --> 00:16:35,163
tell me...

195
00:16:36,330 --> 00:16:38,291
the honest truth.

196
00:16:38,457 --> 00:16:41,002
Tell me it's stomach cancer.
- No.

197
00:16:41,502 --> 00:16:44,589
As I just said,
it's a mild ulcer.

198
00:17:06,027 --> 00:17:07,904
What about surgery?

199
00:17:08,070 --> 00:17:10,198
Can't you operate?

200
00:17:10,364 --> 00:17:14,035
No need for surgery.
It can be treated medically.

201
00:17:14,827 --> 00:17:17,288
What about my diet?

202
00:17:17,872 --> 00:17:19,874
Well, let's see.

203
00:17:20,208 --> 00:17:22,043
Use common sense.

204
00:17:22,210 --> 00:17:25,546
If it's easy to digest,
you can eat whatever you like.

205
00:17:39,268 --> 00:17:41,229
Has he got a year?

206
00:17:41,395 --> 00:17:43,898
No, no more than six months.

207
00:17:44,065 --> 00:17:46,025
- Six months?
- Yeah.

208
00:17:47,276 --> 00:17:49,278
What would you do

209
00:17:49,403 --> 00:17:52,782
if you only had
six months left to live?

210
00:17:58,371 --> 00:18:01,290
Miss Aihara,
what about you?

211
00:18:02,959 --> 00:18:05,127
See the Veronal
in the cabinet?

212
00:19:15,448 --> 00:19:16,949
Is there a blackout?

213
00:19:17,074 --> 00:19:19,827
The street lights are on,
and the neighbors' lights.

214
00:19:19,952 --> 00:19:22,663
Strange.
I wonder if Father is out.

215
00:19:22,830 --> 00:19:25,791
Where's the key?
- In your handbag.

216
00:19:27,501 --> 00:19:30,546
Did Mrs. Hayashi
leave it unlocked?

217
00:19:30,671 --> 00:19:32,381
She's so irresponsible.

218
00:19:32,673 --> 00:19:35,176
That's the problem
with a part-time maid.

219
00:19:35,801 --> 00:19:38,346
Having her live in
wouldn't cost much,

220
00:19:38,512 --> 00:19:40,639
but a burglary sure would.

221
00:19:40,765 --> 00:19:43,976
That's Dad's mentality.
Typical petty bureaucrat.

222
00:19:45,728 --> 00:19:49,857
It's freezing.
Just as bad inside as out.

223
00:19:50,024 --> 00:19:52,193
That's why I hate
Japanese houses.

224
00:19:52,360 --> 00:19:54,779
It's such a drag
coming home to this.

225
00:19:54,945 --> 00:19:57,406
I'd like a modern house.

226
00:19:57,573 --> 00:19:59,158
Honey...

227
00:19:59,450 --> 00:20:03,329
a house of our own would cost
around 500,000 yen, right?

228
00:20:04,080 --> 00:20:06,999
Could we use your dad's
retirement bonus as collateral?

229
00:20:07,124 --> 00:20:10,169
It must be worth
700,000 by now,

230
00:20:10,336 --> 00:20:13,506
plus a monthly pension
of 13,000 yen...

231
00:20:13,923 --> 00:20:16,175
and another 100,000
in savings.

232
00:20:16,300 --> 00:20:18,135
But you think he'd agree?

233
00:20:18,260 --> 00:20:20,930
If he doesn't,
we'll say we're moving out.

234
00:20:21,055 --> 00:20:22,807
That'll clinch it.

235
00:20:22,973 --> 00:20:27,228
Even Dad doesn't want to take
that much money to the grave.

236
00:20:36,987 --> 00:20:38,906
Dad, what's the matter?

237
00:20:42,743 --> 00:20:44,829
Nothing.

238
00:20:45,704 --> 00:20:47,415
Nothing at all.

239
00:20:54,088 --> 00:20:56,006
Somethings not right.

240
00:20:57,800 --> 00:20:58,968
Listen...

241
00:20:59,927 --> 00:21:02,388
this is awful.
- What is?

242
00:21:02,638 --> 00:21:05,266
He heard everything.

243
00:21:06,600 --> 00:21:08,644
He's awful.

244
00:21:08,811 --> 00:21:11,647
It may be his house,
but this is our room.

245
00:21:11,814 --> 00:21:15,401
I can't believe he snuck in here
while we were out.

246
00:21:16,360 --> 00:21:20,156
If he needed to talk to us,
he should have said so.

247
00:21:20,531 --> 00:21:23,242
He's too old
to be sulking around.

248
00:22:08,370 --> 00:22:10,915
Stop making that face.

249
00:22:13,167 --> 00:22:15,252
Enough about your dad.

250
00:22:15,419 --> 00:22:18,797
He has his life.
We have ours.

251
00:22:26,096 --> 00:22:27,681
Hold me.

252
00:22:48,661 --> 00:22:49,745
How sad.

253
00:22:50,955 --> 00:22:52,998
She was so young.

254
00:22:53,749 --> 00:22:57,545
And to leave
such a sweet little boy behind.

255
00:22:58,087 --> 00:23:00,714
She must have died
filled with regret.

256
00:23:00,881 --> 00:23:04,510
Would you stop?
You're like a broken record.

257
00:23:20,317 --> 00:23:23,237
Hurry!
Mommy's getting away!

258
00:23:44,800 --> 00:23:46,510
Whenever
remarriage comes up,

259
00:23:46,677 --> 00:23:49,221
you always
use the boy as an excuse.

260
00:23:49,388 --> 00:23:51,932
When Mitsuo grows up,

261
00:23:52,224 --> 00:23:55,060
he won't care about you
as much as you think.

262
00:23:55,227 --> 00:23:58,647
And when he gets married,
they'll push you aside.

263
00:23:58,856 --> 00:24:01,942
You have to think
about your own future.

264
00:24:02,109 --> 00:24:05,613
I'm telling you:
Find a good wife now.

265
00:24:05,779 --> 00:24:07,740
Even my old lady says so.

266
00:24:07,906 --> 00:24:11,076
She imagines
how filthy it must be

267
00:24:11,201 --> 00:24:15,039
with an oily-skinned man like you
living as a widower.

268
00:24:17,583 --> 00:24:19,585
Dad.

269
00:24:29,595 --> 00:24:31,180
Mitsuo.

270
00:24:33,474 --> 00:24:36,602
Good night.
Please lock the front door.

271
00:25:41,041 --> 00:25:42,543
Mitsuo!

272
00:25:48,006 --> 00:25:50,634
How about that?
Great hit, huh?

273
00:25:50,801 --> 00:25:54,346
That batter is my -

274
00:26:01,145 --> 00:26:04,189
Mitsuo...

275
00:26:09,236 --> 00:26:10,821
Stupid idiot!

276
00:26:10,988 --> 00:26:12,906
What was he thinking?

277
00:26:22,416 --> 00:26:24,460
Mitsuo.

278
00:26:30,841 --> 00:26:32,968
Mitsuo, be brave.

279
00:26:33,093 --> 00:26:35,095
It's just your appendix.

280
00:26:35,345 --> 00:26:37,765
It's no worse
than pulling a tooth.

281
00:26:52,112 --> 00:26:54,573
Can't you stay
for the operation, Dad?

282
00:26:54,698 --> 00:26:58,160
I've got...
some other things to do.

283
00:27:17,971 --> 00:27:21,391
Banzai! Banzai!

284
00:27:26,480 --> 00:27:30,526
Banzai! Banzai!

285
00:27:37,407 --> 00:27:38,826
Dad!

286
00:27:40,327 --> 00:27:41,995
Mitsuo...

287
00:30:18,527 --> 00:30:21,446
CERTIFICATE
OF COMMENDATION

288
00:30:21,571 --> 00:30:26,660
FOR 25 YEARS
OF DISTINGUISHED CIVIL SERVICE

289
00:30:31,289 --> 00:30:34,334
Mr. Watanabe left for work
at his usual time.

290
00:30:34,501 --> 00:30:35,919
What?

291
00:30:38,213 --> 00:30:41,341
But nobody has seen him.

292
00:30:41,925 --> 00:30:45,762
It's been five days now.
And he hasn't called in sick.

293
00:30:45,929 --> 00:30:49,725
The subsection chief asked me
to check in on him.

294
00:30:55,063 --> 00:30:57,691
Ma'am.

295
00:31:04,197 --> 00:31:05,991
What? Impossible!

296
00:31:06,116 --> 00:31:09,745
Bu! ifs true.
The man from his office said so.

297
00:31:09,911 --> 00:31:12,039
What could he be up to?

298
00:31:13,040 --> 00:31:16,126
It seems unbelievable,
but it's true.

299
00:31:16,293 --> 00:31:19,796
The people at his house
looked baffled.

300
00:31:20,839 --> 00:31:22,883
That puts me in a fix.

301
00:31:23,508 --> 00:31:27,971
Why? I can stamp
any paperwork while he's out.

302
00:31:28,263 --> 00:31:31,433
But he has to approve
any resignations, right?

303
00:31:31,600 --> 00:31:34,519
Why?
Are you looking to quit?

304
00:31:34,686 --> 00:31:36,188
I don't belong here.

305
00:31:47,449 --> 00:31:51,078
Uncle, he even withdrew
50,000 yen.

306
00:31:51,244 --> 00:31:53,288
That miser?

307
00:31:53,997 --> 00:31:55,957
I wonder
if he's got a woman.

308
00:31:56,124 --> 00:31:58,251
That would
really be something.

309
00:31:58,418 --> 00:32:00,712
- Now, dear.
- I'm sure it's not that.

310
00:32:00,837 --> 00:32:03,715
There's no telling
when it comes to love.

311
00:32:04,091 --> 00:32:07,344
The ones you least expect
are the most susceptible.

312
00:32:08,303 --> 00:32:10,138
If you ask me,

313
00:32:10,305 --> 00:32:13,391
he's actually a real lecher.

314
00:32:13,600 --> 00:32:15,852
A lecher
with a solemn veneer.

315
00:32:16,019 --> 00:32:19,397
But he's stayed widowed
these 20 years for you.

316
00:32:19,606 --> 00:32:22,651
It makes sense that
he'd eventually snap, right?

317
00:32:22,818 --> 00:32:26,780
Nonsense. He's lost
a lot of weight recently,

318
00:32:26,947 --> 00:32:29,741
and his oily skin
has gotten rough and dry.

319
00:32:29,950 --> 00:32:31,910
There must be
another explanation.

320
00:32:32,077 --> 00:32:33,620
You saw him recently?

321
00:32:33,787 --> 00:32:37,499
Four days ago.
He showed up in the morning.

322
00:32:37,749 --> 00:32:41,670
I thought there was definitely
something wrong.

323
00:32:41,837 --> 00:32:44,464
But your uncle here
blurted out,

324
00:32:44,589 --> 00:32:48,802
“Why so glum? If you've come
for a loan, forget it.”

325
00:32:48,969 --> 00:32:51,513
How could I suspect
a love affair

326
00:32:51,638 --> 00:32:53,682
when he looked like that?

327
00:32:53,807 --> 00:32:55,642
Hush! You see...

328
00:32:55,809 --> 00:33:00,522
my husband thinks all men
are as debauched as he is.

329
00:33:04,776 --> 00:33:09,114
Mitsuo, did anything
happen at home?

330
00:33:10,240 --> 00:33:12,409
Nothing in particular.

331
00:33:36,975 --> 00:33:38,435
HEY -.

332
00:33:39,060 --> 00:33:41,104
Deliver this to my place.

333
00:33:44,232 --> 00:33:46,776
A guy from the magazine
is waiting there.

334
00:33:50,447 --> 00:33:53,783
And get some cyclobarbital
at the pharmacy.

335
00:33:53,950 --> 00:33:55,994
Just give them my name.

336
00:33:56,161 --> 00:33:58,872
But sir, the pharmacy's
already closed.

337
00:33:59,039 --> 00:34:00,707
Is it that late?

338
00:34:00,832 --> 00:34:03,793
In this neighborhood
folks go to bed after dinner.

339
00:34:04,169 --> 00:34:06,004
That stinks.

340
00:34:06,838 --> 00:34:10,800
I can't sleep unless
I chase it down with whisky.

341
00:34:10,967 --> 00:34:14,554
Pardon me for butting in...

342
00:34:16,556 --> 00:34:20,268
but I happen to have some.

343
00:34:37,994 --> 00:34:39,871
Much obliged.

344
00:34:40,580 --> 00:34:43,124
May I pay you
the official price?

345
00:34:43,291 --> 00:34:44,876
No, no.

346
00:34:46,211 --> 00:34:48,922
I was planning
to throw it away.

347
00:34:51,549 --> 00:34:53,927
- But -
- No, really.

348
00:34:54,552 --> 00:34:56,221
Really?

349
00:34:56,846 --> 00:34:59,641
Then let me
pick up your tab.

350
00:34:59,766 --> 00:35:01,559
Oh, no.

351
00:35:01,768 --> 00:35:03,770
You can hold
your liquor, right?

352
00:35:04,437 --> 00:35:07,190
Drink up.
You hardly seem tipsy.

353
00:35:07,816 --> 00:35:09,234
Thank you.

354
00:35:14,489 --> 00:35:17,993
Even if I drink,
I'll just throw it up.

355
00:35:19,369 --> 00:35:21,788
You see...

356
00:35:21,913 --> 00:35:24,499
I have stomach cancer.

357
00:35:25,333 --> 00:35:27,085
- Stomach cancer?
- Yes.

358
00:35:29,921 --> 00:35:31,798
I don't know what to say.

359
00:35:33,466 --> 00:35:37,262
In that case,
what you're doing is crazy.

360
00:35:37,429 --> 00:35:41,474
Yes, I'm ashamed to admit.

361
00:35:42,142 --> 00:35:43,852
I mean...

362
00:35:44,811 --> 00:35:48,773
drinking when you have
stomach cancer is suicide.

363
00:35:52,485 --> 00:35:55,405
The thing is...
I can't go through with it.

364
00:36:00,160 --> 00:36:03,246
“Go ahead and kill yourself,”
I think.

365
00:36:04,205 --> 00:36:06,124
And yet...

366
00:36:06,583 --> 00:36:08,835
I just can't do it.

367
00:36:11,880 --> 00:36:13,506
I mean...

368
00:36:14,799 --> 00:36:17,052
I can't bring myself
to make it final.

369
00:36:18,345 --> 00:36:23,266
But what have I been...
living for all these years?

370
00:36:26,686 --> 00:36:28,355
You don't have children?

371
00:36:33,360 --> 00:36:35,028
Does your stomach hurt?

372
00:36:36,905 --> 00:36:40,825
No, it's not my stomach.

373
00:36:49,250 --> 00:36:53,880
There seems to be
a deeper issue here.

374
00:36:54,089 --> 00:36:55,131
No.

375
00:36:58,760 --> 00:37:00,512
It's just...

376
00:37:01,638 --> 00:37:05,225
that I've been such a fool.

377
00:37:07,685 --> 00:37:09,687
I'm just...

378
00:37:16,277 --> 00:37:18,947
so mad at myself.

379
00:37:22,242 --> 00:37:26,955
Until just a few days ago...

380
00:37:28,289 --> 00:37:32,669
I had never even
bought myself a drink.

381
00:37:34,087 --> 00:37:36,923
It's only now
that I don't know

382
00:37:37,090 --> 00:37:40,343
how much longer
I have to live...

383
00:37:40,802 --> 00:37:42,929
that I've finally begun -

384
00:37:43,096 --> 00:37:44,806
I understand.

385
00:37:45,390 --> 00:37:47,183
I understand.

386
00:37:47,851 --> 00:37:50,895
But drinking now
is plain crazy.

387
00:37:51,938 --> 00:37:54,357
Besides,
does it even taste good?

388
00:37:54,524 --> 00:37:57,193
No, it doesn't.

389
00:37:58,153 --> 00:38:01,239
But for a little while

390
00:38:01,364 --> 00:38:03,992
I can forget my cancer

391
00:38:04,868 --> 00:38:08,455
and all the other
painful things.

392
00:38:09,914 --> 00:38:11,541
This...

393
00:38:15,336 --> 00:38:17,797
this expensive sake...

394
00:38:19,382 --> 00:38:23,178
is like drinking poison...

395
00:38:24,220 --> 00:38:27,390
to get back at myself.

396
00:38:28,224 --> 00:38:29,809
I mean...

397
00:38:31,186 --> 00:38:34,981
that is... it's kind of...

398
00:38:36,733 --> 00:38:39,986
a painful feeling...

399
00:38:45,575 --> 00:38:47,452
I understand.

400
00:38:49,579 --> 00:38:52,165
Sorry to be
telling you all this.

401
00:39:22,987 --> 00:39:25,365
Listen...

402
00:39:26,032 --> 00:39:29,035
I have 50,000 yen on me

403
00:39:29,452 --> 00:39:33,373
that I'd like to spend
all at once.

404
00:39:34,541 --> 00:39:37,877
But I'm ashamed to admit

405
00:39:38,044 --> 00:39:41,631
that I'm not even sure
how to go about it.

406
00:39:42,340 --> 00:39:45,134
What I'm trying to say is -

407
00:39:45,843 --> 00:39:48,721
You want me to show you
how to spend it?

408
00:39:49,264 --> 00:39:50,640
Yes.

409
00:39:50,765 --> 00:39:53,601
I realize
it's terribly forward of me.

410
00:39:57,814 --> 00:39:59,440
But...

411
00:40:02,610 --> 00:40:04,529
This money -

412
00:40:13,746 --> 00:40:17,875
It took me decades
to save up this money.

413
00:40:19,836 --> 00:40:24,549
All the more reason now to -

414
00:40:24,716 --> 00:40:26,384
I understand.

415
00:40:27,010 --> 00:40:29,971
But please put
your money away.

416
00:40:30,138 --> 00:40:31,931
Tonight's on me.

417
00:40:32,056 --> 00:40:33,933
But that's not -

418
00:40:34,225 --> 00:40:36,185
Just leave things to me.

419
00:40:52,452 --> 00:40:54,162
It's fascinating.

420
00:40:54,871 --> 00:40:57,915
I realize it's rude
to call you fascinating,

421
00:40:58,374 --> 00:41:01,794
but you're
an extremely rare individual.

422
00:41:02,670 --> 00:41:05,006
I'm just a slacker

423
00:41:05,173 --> 00:41:07,467
who writes second-rate fiction.

424
00:41:08,009 --> 00:41:10,553
You've really
started me thinking.

425
00:41:24,025 --> 00:41:27,487
They say there's
something noble about suffering,

426
00:41:27,654 --> 00:41:29,322
and it's true.

427
00:41:29,489 --> 00:41:32,408
Misfortune
teaches us the truth.

428
00:41:32,533 --> 00:41:36,371
Your cancer has opened
your eyes to your own life.

429
00:41:44,170 --> 00:41:47,048
People are fickle
and shallow.

430
00:41:47,924 --> 00:41:50,927
We only realize
how beautiful life is

431
00:41:51,094 --> 00:41:53,221
when we face death.

432
00:41:53,554 --> 00:41:56,724
And even then,
few of us realize it.

433
00:41:56,891 --> 00:42:00,269
The worst among us know
nothing of life until they die.

434
00:42:00,478 --> 00:42:04,607
You're amazing! Rebelling
against your past self at this age!

435
00:42:04,732 --> 00:42:07,193
That rebellious spirit moves me.

436
00:42:07,443 --> 00:42:10,029
You were a slave
to your own life.

437
00:42:10,154 --> 00:42:12,532
Now you'll become
its master.

438
00:42:12,699 --> 00:42:16,119
It's our human duty
to enjoy life.

439
00:42:16,285 --> 00:42:19,497
Wasting it is desecrating
God's great gift.

440
00:42:19,622 --> 00:42:22,709
We have to be greedy for life.

441
00:42:22,875 --> 00:42:26,170
They say greed is a vice,
but that's outdated.

442
00:42:26,337 --> 00:42:30,383
Greed is a virtue,
especially greed for enjoying life.

443
00:42:31,384 --> 00:42:33,052
Come on.

444
00:42:36,848 --> 00:42:40,309
Let's go reclaim
the life you've wasted.

445
00:42:40,518 --> 00:42:42,854
Tonight it will be
my pleasure

446
00:42:42,979 --> 00:42:45,314
to act
as your Mephistopheles,

447
00:42:45,440 --> 00:42:48,818
but a beneficent one
who won't ask for your soul.

448
00:42:51,654 --> 00:42:54,240
How perfect!
There's even a black dog.

449
00:42:54,407 --> 00:42:56,200
Lead the way!

450
00:43:00,580 --> 00:43:01,748
Listen.

451
00:43:01,873 --> 00:43:04,459
These silver balls are you.

452
00:43:04,584 --> 00:43:06,961
They're your life itself.

453
00:43:07,086 --> 00:43:11,048
People strangle themselves
in their daily lives.

454
00:43:11,215 --> 00:43:13,301
This machine sets them free.

455
00:43:13,468 --> 00:43:16,304
It's a vending machine
for dreams and aspirations.

456
00:44:26,499 --> 00:44:28,334
No, don't!

457
00:44:28,501 --> 00:44:30,211
You wouldn't know it,

458
00:44:30,378 --> 00:44:32,964
but these women are
the greediest of all mammals.

459
00:44:35,842 --> 00:44:40,012
It'll cost you a dozen hats
to get that old one back.

460
00:44:46,310 --> 00:44:48,938
Besides,
you're a new man now.

461
00:44:49,105 --> 00:44:51,107
Time to buy a new hat!

462
00:45:13,379 --> 00:45:14,922
Welcome.

463
00:45:17,216 --> 00:45:20,469
Welcome.
It's been a long time!

464
00:45:28,227 --> 00:45:30,354
The same for your friend?

465
00:45:43,075 --> 00:45:45,077
What are you laughing at?

466
00:45:45,244 --> 00:45:48,372
It's the honest truth.
He really does have cancer.

467
00:45:48,748 --> 00:45:51,292
- Then why's he drinking?
- Idiot!

468
00:45:51,459 --> 00:45:54,128
That's why
you'll never understand.

469
00:45:54,337 --> 00:45:57,798
Ecce homo.
“Behold the man.”

470
00:45:58,549 --> 00:46:01,135
This man bears
a cross called cancer.

471
00:46:01,302 --> 00:46:02,929
He's Christ.

472
00:46:03,095 --> 00:46:06,933
If you were diagnosed with cancer,
you'd start dying right away.

473
00:46:07,433 --> 00:46:09,769
But not this fellow.

474
00:46:10,436 --> 00:46:12,855
That's when he started living.

475
00:46:13,105 --> 00:46:14,941
Isn't that right?

476
00:47:06,659 --> 00:47:09,161
Hey, don't stare like that.

477
00:47:09,328 --> 00:47:11,330
It's rude.

478
00:47:11,455 --> 00:47:13,374
This way.

479
00:48:36,874 --> 00:48:40,211
Anyone want
to request a favorite?

480
00:48:41,253 --> 00:48:43,255
“Life is Brief.”

481
00:48:43,380 --> 00:48:44,757
What?

482
00:48:45,925 --> 00:48:47,927
“Life is Brief.”

483
00:48:48,094 --> 00:48:52,389
Fall in love, maidens

484
00:48:52,556 --> 00:48:55,976
That love song
from the nineteen teens? Okay!

485
00:49:24,672 --> 00:49:32,221
Life is brief

486
00:49:33,347 --> 00:49:39,228
Fall in love, maidens

487
00:49:42,481 --> 00:49:50,281
Before the crimson bloom

488
00:49:51,490 --> 00:49:57,037
Fades from your lips

489
00:50:01,292 --> 00:50:09,425
Before the tides of passion

490
00:50:09,800 --> 00:50:15,681
Cool within you

491
00:50:18,684 --> 00:50:25,816
For those of you

492
00:50:27,067 --> 00:50:33,199
Who know no tomorrow

493
00:50:36,368 --> 00:50:44,043
Life is brief

494
00:50:45,377 --> 00:50:51,425
Fall in love, maidens

495
00:50:53,385 --> 00:51:01,560
Before the raven tresses

496
00:51:02,436 --> 00:51:08,275
Begin to fade

497
00:51:10,653 --> 00:51:18,410
Before the flames in your hearts

498
00:51:19,578 --> 00:51:25,501
Flicker and die

499
00:51:27,419 --> 00:51:35,552
For those to whom

500
00:51:36,595 --> 00:51:42,768
Today will never return

501
00:51:49,942 --> 00:51:52,194
That's it!
That's the spirit!

502
00:51:54,571 --> 00:52:00,369
Life is brief

503
00:52:46,040 --> 00:52:47,791
Striptease.

504
00:52:47,916 --> 00:52:50,836
It isn't... an art.

505
00:52:51,170 --> 00:52:53,172
It's more than art.

506
00:52:53,339 --> 00:52:55,424
It's more direct.

507
00:52:55,841 --> 00:52:59,011
That female body

508
00:52:59,178 --> 00:53:02,848
gently undulating
up there is a juicy steak,

509
00:53:03,223 --> 00:53:05,851
a glass of liquor,
an injection of camphor,

510
00:53:06,018 --> 00:53:08,562
testosterone,
streptomycin, uranium -

511
00:55:42,591 --> 00:55:44,009
Stop, please.

512
00:55:45,344 --> 00:55:47,346
Hey, stop the car.

513
00:56:01,026 --> 00:56:02,861
Wha... do we have a flat?

514
00:56:02,986 --> 00:56:04,863
I think he's throwing up.

515
00:57:10,637 --> 00:57:14,391
Let's sing something.
I hate feeling blue.

516
00:58:22,834 --> 00:58:24,670
Section Chief!

517
00:58:35,597 --> 00:58:37,307
I thought it was you!

518
00:58:45,732 --> 00:58:48,193
I hardly recognized you
in that new hat.

519
00:58:56,952 --> 00:59:00,080
I'm glad I ran into you.
I was looking for your place.

520
00:59:00,205 --> 00:59:02,124
Are you off to work?

521
00:59:02,332 --> 00:59:04,126
No, I'm -

522
00:59:04,292 --> 00:59:06,336
Do you have your seal?

523
00:59:07,003 --> 00:59:09,798
No, it's back at home.

524
00:59:10,090 --> 00:59:12,509
I want to quit City Hall.

525
00:59:13,218 --> 00:59:16,221
I'm in a rush.
I've found a new job.

526
00:59:17,389 --> 00:59:19,641
Then... come to my house.

527
00:59:32,821 --> 00:59:35,615
- Why are you quitting?
- I'm bored.

528
00:59:35,741 --> 00:59:39,244
It's killing me.
Every day is like the one before.

529
00:59:39,369 --> 00:59:41,788
Nothing new ever happens.

530
00:59:42,581 --> 00:59:46,084
I've put up with it
for a year and half,

531
00:59:46,251 --> 00:59:48,587
but the only novel thing
that's happened

532
00:59:48,754 --> 00:59:51,339
was that you took
five days off...

533
00:59:51,506 --> 00:59:54,342
and now
this new hat of yours.

534
00:59:54,509 --> 00:59:56,052
That's it.

535
01:00:00,807 --> 01:00:04,227
Listen, don't say anything
when Dad gets back.

536
01:00:04,394 --> 01:00:05,896
I have nothing to say.

537
01:00:06,062 --> 01:00:09,441
I'm saying don't take
that carping attitude.

538
01:00:09,691 --> 01:00:12,569
If you hadn't brought up
his retirement bonus -

539
01:00:13,278 --> 01:00:16,782
How self-serving,
blaming it all on me!

540
01:00:17,032 --> 01:00:19,284
You're the one
who brought up his savings

541
01:00:19,493 --> 01:00:22,621
and how even he wouldn't
take it all to the grave.

542
01:00:22,788 --> 01:00:25,332
But I still can't figure out

543
01:00:25,499 --> 01:00:28,293
why that was enough
to make him act like this.

544
01:00:28,460 --> 01:00:30,879
He's never once
stayed out all night.

545
01:00:31,046 --> 01:00:32,756
Let's drop it.

546
01:00:32,923 --> 01:00:35,383
We have no idea
what he's doing,

547
01:00:35,509 --> 01:00:38,720
much less
whether it's our fault.

548
01:00:45,685 --> 01:00:47,646
I'll wait here.

549
01:00:48,355 --> 01:00:50,398
Please come in for a bit.

550
01:00:51,650 --> 01:00:53,109
Honey!

551
01:01:58,466 --> 01:02:00,343
Thirty years.

552
01:02:00,886 --> 01:02:04,764
It kills me to think
of 30 years in that place.

553
01:02:06,349 --> 01:02:08,018
I'm sorry.

554
01:02:08,184 --> 01:02:10,061
No, it's just that...

555
01:02:11,313 --> 01:02:13,773
recently...

556
01:02:15,108 --> 01:02:17,319
every time
I see that award,

557
01:02:17,694 --> 01:02:21,740
it reminds me
of that joke you read to us.

558
01:02:23,408 --> 01:02:26,745
I mean,
it really rang true.

559
01:02:31,416 --> 01:02:34,628
All these 30 years...

560
01:02:38,256 --> 01:02:42,052
what have I been
doing there?

561
01:02:44,137 --> 01:02:47,057
I can't remember
no matter how I try.

562
01:02:49,225 --> 01:02:51,227
All I remember...

563
01:02:51,895 --> 01:02:56,274
is just being busy...

564
01:02:57,275 --> 01:03:01,112
and even then I was bored.

565
01:03:01,738 --> 01:03:03,740
I had you all wrong, sir.

566
01:03:03,907 --> 01:03:05,951
You really do understand!

567
01:03:06,076 --> 01:03:07,786
What a surprise!

568
01:03:16,628 --> 01:03:18,296
That's crazy.

569
01:03:18,463 --> 01:03:21,132
Whatever Uncle says,
I know Dad best.

570
01:03:21,257 --> 01:03:22,384
But -

571
01:03:22,550 --> 01:03:27,097
It's unimaginable,
Dad with such a young girl.

572
01:03:36,398 --> 01:03:39,484
It's not in the right format.

573
01:04:11,641 --> 01:04:14,519
Are you going
to the office?

574
01:04:14,644 --> 01:04:16,688
Yes, I have to submit this.

575
01:04:16,896 --> 01:04:20,108
Could you turn in
my sick-leave form?

576
01:04:21,359 --> 01:04:23,987
Why are you taking
so much time off?

577
01:04:25,238 --> 01:04:28,908
Everyone's talking about it.
They're calling it your “mutation.”

578
01:04:29,701 --> 01:04:31,244
I just...

579
01:04:34,247 --> 01:04:36,249
Are you really sick?

580
01:04:38,126 --> 01:04:40,378
You do look kind of pale.

581
01:04:41,379 --> 01:04:44,549
I just meant -

582
01:04:45,717 --> 01:04:47,886
I didn't think so.

583
01:04:48,845 --> 01:04:50,764
So where do you go

584
01:04:50,889 --> 01:04:53,600
when you pretend
you're going to work?

585
01:04:55,602 --> 01:04:57,562
Don't try to hide it.

586
01:04:59,814 --> 01:05:02,567
But don't you know

587
01:05:02,734 --> 01:05:06,279
that Mr. Sakai came here yesterday
and told them everything?

588
01:05:08,907 --> 01:05:10,200
Who cares, right?

589
01:05:10,366 --> 01:05:12,702
After 30 years
without an absence,

590
01:05:12,827 --> 01:05:15,413
you deserve
at least six months off.

591
01:05:19,501 --> 01:05:22,253
Besides, I'll cover for you.

592
01:05:22,420 --> 01:05:25,298
I'm not like Carp Windsock.
- Carp Windsock?

593
01:05:25,465 --> 01:05:29,552
Yes, Mr. Sakai
is a human carp windsock.

594
01:05:30,053 --> 01:05:34,265
His lips are always flapping,
but he's just hot air inside.

595
01:05:36,226 --> 01:05:39,312
Plus he always acts
like such a big shot.

596
01:05:39,479 --> 01:05:43,024
He makes 200 yen more
a month than I do,

597
01:05:43,149 --> 01:05:45,944
so he looks down on me.

598
01:06:06,005 --> 01:06:07,590
Bye, then.

599
01:06:18,268 --> 01:06:20,854
Listen, I'll go with you.

600
01:07:14,532 --> 01:07:16,201
Ma'am?

601
01:07:18,536 --> 01:07:20,330
Ma'am!

602
01:07:21,456 --> 01:07:23,374
You're so lucky.

603
01:07:23,499 --> 01:07:26,377
I wish I could live
in a house like yours.

604
01:07:27,045 --> 01:07:29,422
At our place,
three families share two rooms.

605
01:07:29,589 --> 01:07:31,549
It's like
the Warring States period.

606
01:07:33,218 --> 01:07:35,887
And you have
a wonderful son, right?

607
01:07:36,387 --> 01:07:39,807
Tell me, where do they sell
women's stockings?

608
01:07:39,974 --> 01:07:41,893
You want to buy some?

609
01:07:42,018 --> 01:07:44,729
Western clothing stores
carry them.

610
01:07:46,773 --> 01:07:49,817
For your daughter-in-law, right?

611
01:07:49,943 --> 01:07:52,028
I've heard she's very pretty.

612
01:07:52,654 --> 01:07:55,406
That's according
to Carp Windsock's report.

613
01:08:07,418 --> 01:08:10,713
- Are you all right?
- I'm just so excited!

614
01:08:11,047 --> 01:08:14,008
- They make you that -
- I'm so happy!

615
01:08:14,175 --> 01:08:15,927
To buy them myself,

616
01:08:16,094 --> 01:08:19,722
I'd have to have sardines
for lunch for three months.

617
01:08:20,139 --> 01:08:22,767
But why did you buy them
for me?

618
01:08:22,934 --> 01:08:26,145
Well, yours
had holes in them.

619
01:08:27,146 --> 01:08:32,443
Do the holes in my stockings
make your feet cold?

620
01:08:32,610 --> 01:08:34,862
Don't get me wrong -

621
01:08:35,029 --> 01:08:37,407
I was just kidding.

622
01:08:37,991 --> 01:08:40,743
I know you did it
out of kindness,

623
01:08:41,077 --> 01:08:43,705
but I feel awkward
at times like this.

624
01:08:43,871 --> 01:08:46,791
That's why I made a bad joke.
Forgive me.

625
01:09:07,270 --> 01:09:09,314
Want to hear something good?

626
01:09:09,564 --> 01:09:10,732
Something good?

627
01:09:10,857 --> 01:09:14,861
I got so bored that I gave
everyone at work nicknames.

628
01:09:14,986 --> 01:09:16,612
Wanna hear them?

629
01:09:16,779 --> 01:09:18,823
Sea Slug.
- Sea Slug?

630
01:09:19,032 --> 01:09:20,658
Can you guess?

631
01:09:20,783 --> 01:09:22,994
Someone who's slippery
and evasive.

632
01:09:24,996 --> 01:09:27,123
Mr. Ono,
the subsection chief.

633
01:09:27,415 --> 01:09:29,000
Sea Slug...

634
01:09:31,669 --> 01:09:35,715
Gutter Cover. Someone who's
damp and gloomy all year round.

635
01:09:37,425 --> 01:09:40,094
- Would that be Obara?
- Bingo!

636
01:09:42,013 --> 01:09:44,140
Next: Flypaper.

637
01:09:44,307 --> 01:09:47,602
- Flypaper?
- You know - sticks to everything.

638
01:09:48,227 --> 01:09:50,897
You guessed, right?
Mr. Noguchi!

639
01:09:53,733 --> 01:09:56,194
Next is the floor supervisor,
Mr. Saito.

640
01:09:56,361 --> 01:09:59,030
Can you guess his nickname?

641
01:09:59,781 --> 01:10:01,657
Mr. Saito.

642
01:10:02,283 --> 01:10:04,035
Let's see...

643
01:10:04,619 --> 01:10:08,498
His distinguishing feature
is that he has none.

644
01:10:09,332 --> 01:10:12,377
I don't really know...

645
01:10:13,211 --> 01:10:14,879
Set Menu.

646
01:10:15,004 --> 01:10:17,715
- Set Menu?
- At the cafeteria.

647
01:10:22,678 --> 01:10:24,764
And what about Kimura?

648
01:10:24,931 --> 01:10:28,226
Jelly Noodles.
Timid and quivering.

649
01:10:31,312 --> 01:10:34,232
I had a nickname
for you too.

650
01:10:36,067 --> 01:10:39,695
But I'm not going to tell,
'cause I had you all wrong.

651
01:10:39,862 --> 01:10:43,408
No, I don't mind.
Tell me.

652
01:10:44,200 --> 01:10:46,244
Now I've put my foot in it.

653
01:10:46,369 --> 01:10:49,539
No, it would be worse
if you didn't tell me.

654
01:10:50,081 --> 01:10:52,708
Okay, then:
The Mummy.

655
01:10:56,087 --> 01:10:57,880
Forgive me.

656
01:10:58,840 --> 01:11:01,592
It's all right.

657
01:11:03,094 --> 01:11:06,013
So you mean I'm -

658
01:11:20,695 --> 01:11:23,364
I'll say good-bye here.
Thank you so much.

659
01:11:23,489 --> 01:11:27,910
Do you really have to submit
your resignation today?

660
01:11:29,620 --> 01:11:32,206
If you could
make it tomorrow,

661
01:11:32,373 --> 01:11:34,459
we could spend today...

662
01:13:04,590 --> 01:13:06,551
You're not eating anything.

663
01:13:06,759 --> 01:13:08,594
It's just...

664
01:13:09,720 --> 01:13:11,806
You look so tired.

665
01:13:11,973 --> 01:13:16,269
No, today
was really a lot of fun.

666
01:13:17,395 --> 01:13:20,898
But you snored through
the best part of the movie.

667
01:13:23,359 --> 01:13:25,194
It's just that...

668
01:13:26,279 --> 01:13:28,531
I overdid it a bit last night.

669
01:13:33,744 --> 01:13:35,538
Mr. Kimura...

670
01:13:50,261 --> 01:13:53,889
This is something...

671
01:13:54,932 --> 01:13:57,226
I can't tell anyone.

672
01:13:57,643 --> 01:13:59,937
It's too embarrassing.

673
01:14:00,563 --> 01:14:03,899
You see, the reason...

674
01:14:04,859 --> 01:14:06,819
We worked...

675
01:14:08,613 --> 01:14:11,949
like a mummy
these last 30 years -

676
01:14:14,327 --> 01:14:15,578
Oh, no!

677
01:14:15,745 --> 01:14:19,290
It's not that I mind you

678
01:14:19,665 --> 01:14:21,792
calling me a mummy...

679
01:14:22,668 --> 01:14:25,921
because that's exactly right.

680
01:14:27,840 --> 01:14:30,926
I don't blame you at all.

681
01:14:33,304 --> 01:14:36,974
It's just that the reason

682
01:14:37,558 --> 01:14:40,728
I turned myself
into a mummy -

683
01:14:41,979 --> 01:14:45,566
What I mean is
that I did it all...

684
01:14:46,442 --> 01:14:49,028
for my son's sake.

685
01:14:50,571 --> 01:14:54,033
But as it turns out,

686
01:14:54,492 --> 01:14:56,661
my son...

687
01:14:57,787 --> 01:15:00,206
couldn't care in the least.

688
01:15:03,584 --> 01:15:06,879
But you can't blame it all
on your son.

689
01:15:07,672 --> 01:15:09,298
Isn't that right?

690
01:15:09,465 --> 01:15:13,135
Not unless he asked you
to become a mummy.

691
01:15:26,857 --> 01:15:29,193
Parents are all the same.

692
01:15:29,318 --> 01:15:32,446
My morn gives me
the same line sometimes.

693
01:15:32,613 --> 01:15:35,199
“All the trouble
you've put me through!”

694
01:15:35,366 --> 01:15:37,618
I'm grateful she had me,

695
01:15:37,785 --> 01:15:40,079
but babies don't ask
to be born.

696
01:15:45,042 --> 01:15:48,879
But what happened to make you
bad-mouth your son to me?

697
01:15:52,758 --> 01:15:54,552
It's just...

698
01:15:58,723 --> 01:16:01,100
I knew it!
You still adore him!

699
01:16:41,474 --> 01:16:44,810
No end in sight
for the electricity shortage.

700
01:16:45,019 --> 01:16:47,021
Is that right?

701
01:17:28,687 --> 01:17:32,983
This heat is due to the warmest
ocean currents in 30 years.

702
01:17:36,821 --> 01:17:38,697
Is that a fact?

703
01:17:58,634 --> 01:18:02,012
Actually,
there's something...

704
01:18:03,514 --> 01:18:05,558
I'd like to tell you.

705
01:18:09,311 --> 01:18:12,523
I know I should have
told you sooner,

706
01:18:13,023 --> 01:18:17,236
but it's not
very pleasant business.

707
01:18:17,528 --> 01:18:19,864
Father, I already know.

708
01:18:20,573 --> 01:18:24,201
You see, I discussed it
with Uncle today.

709
01:18:24,577 --> 01:18:28,163
We must address practical matters
to avoid complications later.

710
01:18:28,330 --> 01:18:31,542
For instance, although
we have rights as your heirs,

711
01:18:31,667 --> 01:18:34,545
you need to make those clear
to avoid disputes.

712
01:18:34,712 --> 01:18:37,882
She's taken you for 50,000 yen
in just a few days.

713
01:18:38,007 --> 01:18:40,175
Young girls these days -
- What are you...?

714
01:18:40,301 --> 01:18:42,553
Father! We respect

715
01:18:42,678 --> 01:18:44,430
your free will, all right?

716
01:18:44,555 --> 01:18:46,891
We even accept
your debauchery!

717
01:18:47,057 --> 01:18:49,727
But it's common sense
to set some conditions.

718
01:18:49,894 --> 01:18:52,146
Think of Kazue,

719
01:18:52,271 --> 01:18:54,398
or at least
of her family back home.

720
01:18:54,565 --> 01:18:57,776
How dare you bring home
a woman like that!

721
01:18:57,985 --> 01:19:00,779
And holding hands
with her in your room!

722
01:19:00,905 --> 01:19:04,116
I could hardly face Mrs. Hayashi
when she told me!

723
01:19:41,195 --> 01:19:43,405
Two weeks have passed

724
01:19:43,530 --> 01:19:47,201
since our protagonist
abandoned his post.

725
01:19:57,127 --> 01:19:58,629
During that time,

726
01:19:58,796 --> 01:20:01,924
various rumors
and endless speculation

727
01:20:02,049 --> 01:20:04,885
about Mr. Watanabe
have made the rounds.

728
01:20:19,775 --> 01:20:23,696
The rumors and speculation
all agree on one thing:

729
01:20:23,862 --> 01:20:26,323
Whatever Mr. Watanabe
is doing,

730
01:20:26,490 --> 01:20:28,784
it is very frivolous indeed.

731
01:20:30,661 --> 01:20:32,663
But in point of fact,

732
01:20:32,788 --> 01:20:35,624
Watanabe
had never been this earnest

733
01:20:35,791 --> 01:20:38,127
about anything
in his entire life.

734
01:20:39,837 --> 01:20:41,672
This isn't City Hall,

735
01:20:41,839 --> 01:20:46,510
where you can waste
a whole day on an hour's work!

736
01:20:46,677 --> 01:20:48,178
I just -

737
01:20:48,345 --> 01:20:51,015
Every second I waste here
costs me money.

738
01:20:51,181 --> 01:20:53,517
Then what about tonight?

739
01:20:53,684 --> 01:20:57,021
I'm pooped at night.
I'd rather sleep than go out.

740
01:20:59,732 --> 01:21:02,818
Besides, why are you
always taking me out?

741
01:21:02,985 --> 01:21:04,236
It's just -

742
01:21:05,029 --> 01:21:08,615
Let's stop doing this.
It doesn't feel right.

743
01:21:08,991 --> 01:21:12,870
Just one last time tonight.

744
01:21:13,037 --> 01:21:15,414
No, or it'll never end!

745
01:21:16,665 --> 01:21:18,292
Now, excuse me.

746
01:21:46,403 --> 01:21:48,530
Tonight's the last time, okay?

747
01:22:51,260 --> 01:22:53,637
Shall we go for a stroll?

748
01:22:53,804 --> 01:22:55,347
I've had enough.

749
01:22:55,472 --> 01:22:59,434
Next it'll be a sweets shop,
then a sushi or noodle joint.

750
01:23:00,394 --> 01:23:03,230
What's the point
of these endless rounds?

751
01:23:04,189 --> 01:23:07,359
I feel bad saying this
when you pay for everything,

752
01:23:07,484 --> 01:23:09,486
but I've had enough.

753
01:23:09,778 --> 01:23:13,157
We've run out of things
to talk about.

754
01:23:17,035 --> 01:23:19,246
There's that face again.

755
01:23:20,789 --> 01:23:24,042
The truth is
you give me the creeps.

756
01:23:34,344 --> 01:23:37,973
What's going on? Why do you
chase me around like this?

757
01:23:38,307 --> 01:23:41,185
- It's just -
- It's just what?

758
01:23:41,518 --> 01:23:43,187
It's just -

759
01:23:43,353 --> 01:23:47,482
I enjoy spending time
with you this way.

760
01:23:47,649 --> 01:23:49,651
A May-December romance?
Forget it.

761
01:23:49,818 --> 01:23:51,278
No, no!

762
01:23:51,862 --> 01:23:53,655
It's just that I -

763
01:23:53,822 --> 01:23:57,951
Just spit it out
instead of sputtering like that.

764
01:24:02,915 --> 01:24:05,000
- Are you mad?
- No.

765
01:24:13,050 --> 01:24:17,554
I don't even know myself...

766
01:24:18,472 --> 01:24:22,351
why I keep
following you around.

767
01:24:23,810 --> 01:24:27,189
All I know is -

768
01:24:50,587 --> 01:24:52,005
Listen:

769
01:24:52,547 --> 01:24:54,591
I'm going to die soon.

770
01:24:55,425 --> 01:24:58,512
I've got stomach cancer.
It's right here.

771
01:25:02,683 --> 01:25:05,769
Do you see?

772
01:25:06,770 --> 01:25:08,772
No matter how I struggle,

773
01:25:08,939 --> 01:25:11,858
I've only got six months
to a year left.

774
01:25:12,693 --> 01:25:15,279
When I found out,

775
01:25:15,445 --> 01:25:17,990
I suddenly... in here -

776
01:25:18,615 --> 01:25:20,117
Now I remember:

777
01:25:20,284 --> 01:25:24,788
I nearly drowned in a pond once
when I was a child.

778
01:25:24,997 --> 01:25:27,332
I felt exactly
the same way then.

779
01:25:27,499 --> 01:25:29,751
Everything's going black.

780
01:25:30,043 --> 01:25:32,296
I writhe and thrash around,

781
01:25:32,796 --> 01:25:35,465
but there's nothing
to hold on to...

782
01:25:35,632 --> 01:25:38,176
except you.
- What about your son?

783
01:25:38,343 --> 01:25:40,470
Don't talk to me about him!

784
01:25:41,638 --> 01:25:43,640
I have no son.

785
01:25:45,309 --> 01:25:47,728
I'm all alone.
- But -

786
01:25:47,936 --> 01:25:50,480
No, you don't understand.

787
01:25:51,940 --> 01:25:55,193
My son
is somewhere far away.

788
01:25:56,945 --> 01:25:58,989
Just as my morn and pop were

789
01:25:59,156 --> 01:26:02,993
when I was drowning
in that pond.

790
01:26:03,702 --> 01:26:05,495
Remembering it now,

791
01:26:05,954 --> 01:26:08,999
it's even more painful
than it was then.

792
01:26:09,750 --> 01:26:12,753
But why someone like me?

793
01:26:12,961 --> 01:26:15,047
It's just that...

794
01:26:15,672 --> 01:26:19,259
when I look at you,
I get a warm feeling...

795
01:26:19,426 --> 01:26:22,137
right here.

796
01:26:22,679 --> 01:26:26,224
Even an old mummy like me.

797
01:26:33,190 --> 01:26:36,777
What I mean is,
you've been really kind to me.

798
01:26:37,569 --> 01:26:39,738
No, that's not it.

799
01:26:39,905 --> 01:26:43,450
You're young and healthy,
and that's why -

800
01:26:47,037 --> 01:26:48,705
No.

801
01:26:48,997 --> 01:26:50,749
I mean...

802
01:26:52,084 --> 01:26:55,879
what makes you so lively?

803
01:26:56,046 --> 01:26:58,048
You're just so full of life.

804
01:26:58,215 --> 01:27:00,634
That's why I -

805
01:27:00,801 --> 01:27:03,720
That's why this mummy
envies you!

806
01:27:04,888 --> 01:27:08,600
Before I die,
even if only for one day,

807
01:27:08,767 --> 01:27:11,812
I want to be like that.

808
01:27:12,020 --> 01:27:15,899
Until I've done that,
I can't let myself die.

809
01:27:16,066 --> 01:27:18,068
You see, I just...

810
01:27:18,235 --> 01:27:21,071
I just want something to -

811
01:27:21,238 --> 01:27:23,240
I want to do something.

812
01:27:23,448 --> 01:27:27,077
I just don't know what.

813
01:27:27,244 --> 01:27:29,246
But you know.

814
01:27:29,413 --> 01:27:31,748
No, maybe you don't, but -

815
01:27:31,915 --> 01:27:33,291
But I don't!

816
01:27:33,458 --> 01:27:35,919
Tell me,
how can I be like you?

817
01:27:36,086 --> 01:27:39,881
- But all I do is work and eat!
- What else?

818
01:27:40,006 --> 01:27:41,842
That's all!

819
01:27:48,432 --> 01:27:49,975
It's true.

820
01:27:50,100 --> 01:27:52,853
All I do is make
these little things.

821
01:28:02,279 --> 01:28:04,865
Just making these
is so much fun.

822
01:28:05,031 --> 01:28:09,035
I feel like I'm making friends
with every baby in Japan.

823
01:28:11,246 --> 01:28:14,040
Why don't you try
making something too?

824
01:28:15,250 --> 01:28:18,211
What could I possibly make
at that office?

825
01:28:19,588 --> 01:28:22,382
Yeah, it's hopeless there.

826
01:28:22,549 --> 01:28:25,469
You should quit
and go somewhere...

827
01:28:33,727 --> 01:28:35,979
It's too late.

828
01:29:10,013 --> 01:29:13,016
No... it's not too late.

829
01:29:15,560 --> 01:29:17,229
It's not hopeless.

830
01:29:17,395 --> 01:29:19,898
Even there,
there's something I can do.

831
01:29:20,065 --> 01:29:22,192
I just have to find the will.

832
01:29:30,408 --> 01:29:34,162
There's something I can do.

833
01:30:11,074 --> 01:30:14,202
It's only a matter of time
before he resigns.

834
01:30:14,369 --> 01:30:17,247
His son came by yesterday
about his retirement bonus.

835
01:30:17,581 --> 01:30:20,584
Which means
you'll finally be section chief.

836
01:30:20,709 --> 01:30:22,919
Well, you never know.

837
01:31:05,920 --> 01:31:07,589
Good morning.

838
01:31:18,266 --> 01:31:19,476
Section Chief.

839
01:31:23,271 --> 01:31:26,524
Ono, take care of this.

840
01:31:28,610 --> 01:31:30,695
PETITION TO REPAIR
AND FILL IN CULVERT

841
01:31:30,862 --> 01:31:33,365
KUROE WOMEN'S ASSOCIATION

842
01:31:33,531 --> 01:31:37,285
TO BE ASSIGNED
TO PUBLIC WORKS

843
01:31:37,494 --> 01:31:39,663
Is this really a job
for Public Works?

844
01:31:39,829 --> 01:31:43,333
Actually, this is
precisely the kind of job

845
01:31:43,917 --> 01:31:48,380
that Public Affairs
should oversee.

846
01:31:48,630 --> 01:31:51,341
Public Works
can't do it alone.

847
01:31:51,508 --> 01:31:54,928
Parks and Sewage
must also take action.

848
01:31:57,514 --> 01:32:00,058
Get me a car, will you?

849
01:32:00,475 --> 01:32:03,103
I'm going out
to survey the site.

850
01:32:04,187 --> 01:32:06,690
Put together a report today.

851
01:32:06,856 --> 01:32:09,317
Sir, I'm not sure
that's possible.

852
01:32:09,484 --> 01:32:12,195
It is if you set your mind to it.

853
01:32:36,219 --> 01:32:38,638
Five months later.

854
01:32:38,888 --> 01:32:42,225
The protagonist
of our story has died.

855
01:33:27,645 --> 01:33:30,774
Let us see the deputy mayor.
He must be here.

856
01:33:31,065 --> 01:33:33,610
Just five minutes, please.

857
01:33:45,371 --> 01:33:48,082
Reporters.
What should I tell them?

858
01:34:14,067 --> 01:34:18,154
- Your suspicions are groundless.
- Are they?

859
01:34:18,321 --> 01:34:21,157
Our claim is based
on thorough research.

860
01:34:21,324 --> 01:34:23,034
Sir, the official story

861
01:34:23,159 --> 01:34:25,995
is that the Parks Department
and local councilman

862
01:34:26,246 --> 01:34:28,998
built that park
with your backing,

863
01:34:29,165 --> 01:34:32,001
but wasn't it really
Mr. Watanabe?

864
01:34:32,168 --> 01:34:33,628
That's the word.

865
01:34:33,753 --> 01:34:36,965
Mr. Watanabe
was chief of Public Affairs.

866
01:34:37,131 --> 01:34:40,301
Building parks is
the Park Department's job.

867
01:34:40,468 --> 01:34:42,136
We understand that.

868
01:34:42,303 --> 01:34:45,390
But the plan almost
fizzled out several times.

869
01:34:45,557 --> 01:34:48,685
We're talking about the man
who saw it to completion.

870
01:34:48,852 --> 01:34:52,480
Local residents believe that
that was Mr. Watanabe.

871
01:34:52,647 --> 01:34:54,858
They're deeply concerned
about the fact

872
01:34:55,024 --> 01:34:57,360
that he died
in the park he built.

873
01:34:57,527 --> 01:34:59,529
What's that supposed
to mean?

874
01:34:59,654 --> 01:35:03,366
Well, they were suspicious
even before that happened.

875
01:35:03,533 --> 01:35:07,662
For example, in your speech
at the park's opening ceremony,

876
01:35:07,829 --> 01:35:10,707
you never mentioned
Mr. Watanabe once.

877
01:35:10,874 --> 01:35:13,167
They say it wasn't
a congratulatory address.

878
01:35:13,334 --> 01:35:14,752
Then what was it?

879
01:35:14,919 --> 01:35:16,921
A campaign speech.

880
01:35:19,591 --> 01:35:21,342
Furthermore...

881
01:35:21,509 --> 01:35:24,137
he was completely ignored
during the ceremony

882
01:35:24,304 --> 01:35:26,389
and seated
in the back corner.

883
01:35:26,556 --> 01:35:30,685
In short, people feel
a lot of sympathy for the guy.

884
01:35:31,060 --> 01:35:33,354
So the fact that
he died in the park

885
01:35:33,521 --> 01:35:35,607
has led to some
interesting speculation.

886
01:35:36,649 --> 01:35:40,486
It's seen
as an act of silent protest

887
01:35:40,653 --> 01:35:42,697
against top city officials.

888
01:35:42,822 --> 01:35:47,201
So you're saying
Watanabe committed suicide

889
01:35:47,327 --> 01:35:51,706
or at least let himself
freeze to death?

890
01:35:52,874 --> 01:35:54,500
More or less.

891
01:35:55,460 --> 01:35:56,920
I see.

892
01:35:57,086 --> 01:35:59,756
Don't forget it was also
snowing last night.

893
01:35:59,923 --> 01:36:02,884
Sounds like a scene
right out of a play.

894
01:36:03,009 --> 01:36:04,052
However...

895
01:36:05,136 --> 01:36:08,389
an autopsy
has clearly established

896
01:36:08,514 --> 01:36:11,142
the cause
of Watanabe's death.

897
01:36:11,392 --> 01:36:15,396
He certainly didn't commit suicide.
Nor did he freeze to death.

898
01:36:15,897 --> 01:36:18,733
He died of stomach cancer.
- Stomach cancer?

899
01:36:19,067 --> 01:36:21,945
That's right.
Internal hemorrhaging.

900
01:36:22,445 --> 01:36:26,616
Watanabe died suddenly,
without warning.

901
01:36:27,200 --> 01:36:28,993
If you have any doubts -

902
01:36:29,160 --> 01:36:32,914
Ono, give them
the name of the hospital.

903
01:37:47,363 --> 01:37:49,282
It's just not right.

904
01:37:50,992 --> 01:37:54,370
The way these reporters think,
and their tactlessness -

905
01:37:54,871 --> 01:37:56,330
Actually...

906
01:37:56,497 --> 01:37:59,417
it applies
to the general public too.

907
01:38:00,334 --> 01:38:05,631
They just don't understand
how government works.

908
01:38:09,719 --> 01:38:12,889
They're woefully ignorant
of how we function.

909
01:38:13,222 --> 01:38:16,642
Take that little park in Kuroe.

910
01:38:17,143 --> 01:38:19,937
Some seem to believe

911
01:38:20,104 --> 01:38:22,106
that Watanabe built it,

912
01:38:22,231 --> 01:38:24,400
but that's ridiculous.

913
01:38:24,901 --> 01:38:27,195
Maybe I shouldn't say this

914
01:38:28,654 --> 01:38:31,074
here at his wake,

915
01:38:31,240 --> 01:38:34,911
in front of his son
and relatives...

916
01:38:35,912 --> 01:38:37,997
but I'll go ahead,

917
01:38:38,122 --> 01:38:41,209
because I think Watanabe
would agree with me.

918
01:38:42,293 --> 01:38:46,547
Certainly, Watanabe
went to great pains

919
01:38:47,006 --> 01:38:50,009
to get that park built.

920
01:38:50,426 --> 01:38:53,971
I take my hat off
to his enthusiasm.

921
01:38:54,764 --> 01:38:59,060
But his efforts were part
of his official duties.

922
01:39:02,939 --> 01:39:06,442
The idea he went beyond
the scope of his office

923
01:39:06,609 --> 01:39:08,820
to facilitate citizens' desires

924
01:39:09,028 --> 01:39:12,073
and actually built
the park himself

925
01:39:12,240 --> 01:39:15,493
is nonsense to those
who understand our bureaucracy.

926
01:39:18,496 --> 01:39:22,083
Watanabe himself
would grimace at the idea.

927
01:39:27,797 --> 01:39:29,465
However...

928
01:39:30,633 --> 01:39:33,386
we may be partly to blame

929
01:39:33,636 --> 01:39:36,931
for the fact
people are saying such things.

930
01:39:37,223 --> 01:39:39,684
All eyes were on
that epoch-making project

931
01:39:39,851 --> 01:39:42,145
as it was rushed
to completion.

932
01:39:42,311 --> 01:39:45,815
Maybe we should have
singled out someone's service.

933
01:39:46,232 --> 01:39:47,775
For instance...

934
01:39:48,276 --> 01:39:50,570
the chief
of the Parks Department.

935
01:39:51,320 --> 01:39:53,656
Or his superior...

936
01:39:54,824 --> 01:39:57,034
the chief of Public Works.

937
01:39:58,786 --> 01:40:02,248
If you say so,
Mr. Deputy Mayor,

938
01:40:02,373 --> 01:40:04,500
but here's what I think.

939
01:40:04,667 --> 01:40:07,920
The head
of the Parks Department and I

940
01:40:08,087 --> 01:40:10,590
only fulfilled the duties
of our offices.

941
01:40:10,798 --> 01:40:14,343
But when I think
how difficult it was

942
01:40:14,635 --> 01:40:19,223
to navigate the intricate politics
of the city council

943
01:40:19,390 --> 01:40:22,185
and realize
the park's construction,

944
01:40:22,351 --> 01:40:26,147
it is you, Deputy Mayor,
who should be singled out.

945
01:40:27,273 --> 01:40:29,150
Now, now...

946
01:40:30,359 --> 01:40:33,029
Some people
have even criticized

947
01:40:33,154 --> 01:40:35,364
my speech
at the opening ceremony.

948
01:40:35,573 --> 01:40:37,241
Isn't that right, Ono?

949
01:40:37,408 --> 01:40:40,453
They called it
a campaign speech!

950
01:40:43,372 --> 01:40:47,293
Excuse me.
The residents of Kuroe

951
01:40:47,501 --> 01:40:51,214
are here asking to burn incense
for the deceased.

952
01:42:56,881 --> 01:42:59,842
Kiichi, come.
You too, Mitsuo.

953
01:45:04,258 --> 01:45:06,218
It sure is cold.

954
01:45:06,969 --> 01:45:08,846
May I pour one for you?

955
01:45:10,681 --> 01:45:12,892
I'll get some warm sake.

956
01:45:13,309 --> 01:45:15,144
What do you say?

957
01:45:15,269 --> 01:45:18,814
Why don't you all move
closer down here?

958
01:45:18,939 --> 01:45:20,399
Why not?

959
01:45:22,151 --> 01:45:24,069
- Over here, please.
- Thanks.

960
01:45:24,737 --> 01:45:26,989
Mr. Hayashi.

961
01:45:42,671 --> 01:45:44,089
Mr. Ono.

962
01:45:46,675 --> 01:45:49,553
Did all the big shots
go off to a meeting?

963
01:45:49,803 --> 01:45:51,222
Yes.

964
01:45:52,223 --> 01:45:55,184
No, they just couldn't stand
to stay here.

965
01:45:57,269 --> 01:45:59,313
I don't care
what anyone says.

966
01:45:59,438 --> 01:46:02,733
It was Mr. Watanabe
who built that park.

967
01:46:03,317 --> 01:46:06,904
The deputy mayor
and his people know it.

968
01:46:07,321 --> 01:46:11,075
That's going too far.
As the deputy mayor said -

969
01:46:11,200 --> 01:46:12,701
That's right.

970
01:46:12,826 --> 01:46:15,829
I'm not saying this because
I'm in the Parks Department,

971
01:46:15,955 --> 01:46:19,416
but we drew up the plans
and oversaw construction.

972
01:46:19,875 --> 01:46:24,004
- That's not what I meant.
- Now, now...

973
01:46:24,588 --> 01:46:27,049
I understand
how you feel, but -

974
01:46:27,216 --> 01:46:30,094
The point is
he was in Public Affairs.

975
01:46:30,261 --> 01:46:34,056
Did he think
he could just build a park?

976
01:46:34,223 --> 01:46:36,433
We have to respect
each other's turf or -

977
01:46:36,600 --> 01:46:39,853
If I had to say
how that park got built,

978
01:46:40,020 --> 01:46:42,731
I'd say it was coincidence.

979
01:46:42,898 --> 01:46:44,567
Hear me out.

980
01:46:44,733 --> 01:46:49,238
If a looming election hadn't put
pressure on the local councilman,

981
01:46:50,030 --> 01:46:52,324
and if profiteers hadn't lobbied

982
01:46:52,449 --> 01:46:54,994
to build
a red-light district there,

983
01:46:55,119 --> 01:46:58,956
that reclamation work
wouldn't have proceeded so quickly.

984
01:46:59,081 --> 01:47:00,457
That's right.

985
01:47:00,624 --> 01:47:02,668
Exactly right.

986
01:47:03,752 --> 01:47:06,422
There's one thing
I can't figure out.

987
01:47:07,756 --> 01:47:12,219
Why would
someone like him

988
01:47:12,386 --> 01:47:15,264
suddenly change like that?

989
01:47:16,932 --> 01:47:19,059
What could it have been?

990
01:47:22,938 --> 01:47:25,149
I agree.
It's a complete mystery.

991
01:47:25,316 --> 01:47:27,067
It really is.

992
01:47:27,359 --> 01:47:29,778
I mean...

993
01:47:29,945 --> 01:47:32,531
with the benefit of hindsight,

994
01:47:32,906 --> 01:47:35,868
it would seem he knew

995
01:47:35,993 --> 01:47:38,662
he had stomach cancer,
and that's why -

996
01:47:39,705 --> 01:47:41,999
We were just discussing

997
01:47:42,124 --> 01:47:45,336
whether your father knew
he had stomach cancer.

998
01:47:45,502 --> 01:47:48,881
Well, I think he would have
told me if he did.

999
01:47:49,048 --> 01:47:50,174
Indeed.

1000
01:47:50,341 --> 01:47:53,260
I believe my father
was fortunate to die

1001
01:47:53,385 --> 01:47:55,888
without realizing
he had cancer.

1002
01:47:56,055 --> 01:47:58,766
A diagnosis like that
is a death sentence.

1003
01:47:58,932 --> 01:48:00,267
I see.

1004
01:48:00,392 --> 01:48:02,978
Well, that rules out
Saito's theory.

1005
01:48:03,228 --> 01:48:05,105
What theory is that?

1006
01:48:05,439 --> 01:48:09,485
Well, five months ago he went
through a sudden transformation.

1007
01:48:09,652 --> 01:48:11,320
That's for sure.

1008
01:48:11,445 --> 01:48:15,449
And none of us has
any clue what caused it.

1009
01:48:15,824 --> 01:48:18,410
Well, that was
a woman's doing.

1010
01:48:18,827 --> 01:48:21,330
With a young mistress,

1011
01:48:21,497 --> 01:48:25,334
the hormonal effect can temporarily
rejuvenate an old man.

1012
01:48:25,501 --> 01:48:27,503
Happens all the time.

1013
01:48:27,670 --> 01:48:30,172
It suddenly put a sheen
back on his cheeks.

1014
01:48:30,339 --> 01:48:32,716
Actually...

1015
01:48:32,883 --> 01:48:36,136
we heard rumors
at that time of a romance.

1016
01:48:36,553 --> 01:48:39,056
That explains
the rakish hat.

1017
01:48:39,223 --> 01:48:40,599
Sure does.

1018
01:48:40,766 --> 01:48:43,686
That surprised
the hell out of me.

1019
01:48:47,064 --> 01:48:50,067
Sir, I'm not sure
that's possible.

1020
01:48:50,234 --> 01:48:52,986
It is if you set your mind to it.

1021
01:49:03,706 --> 01:49:05,290
But wait.

1022
01:49:40,325 --> 01:49:42,286
In any case,

1023
01:49:42,453 --> 01:49:46,707
his enthusiasm
was a bit over the top.

1024
01:49:46,874 --> 01:49:49,251
Yes. That's very true.

1025
01:49:50,252 --> 01:49:52,171
I don't mean to argue,

1026
01:49:52,296 --> 01:49:56,133
but a woman alone
can't account for -

1027
01:49:56,258 --> 01:49:57,926
- But -
- Dear.

1028
01:49:58,093 --> 01:50:00,429
The thing is...

1029
01:50:03,098 --> 01:50:05,267
it just doesn't make sense.

1030
01:50:09,271 --> 01:50:12,441
Not to change the subject,

1031
01:50:12,775 --> 01:50:16,111
but there were times
when his extraordinary zeal

1032
01:50:16,236 --> 01:50:18,405
threatened to derail it all.

1033
01:50:18,781 --> 01:50:22,284
That's city hall for you.
Gotta guard your turf.

1034
01:50:22,451 --> 01:50:26,038
That's right.
The biggest mystery to me

1035
01:50:26,205 --> 01:50:29,708
is how a 30-year veteran
could suddenly forget the rules.

1036
01:50:29,875 --> 01:50:31,835
It's because he -

1037
01:50:38,050 --> 01:50:39,593
In any event...

1038
01:50:39,760 --> 01:50:43,305
pushing his plan
on all the other departments

1039
01:50:43,639 --> 01:50:47,309
ensured that everyone
would dig in their heels.

1040
01:50:47,476 --> 01:50:50,062
My boss was
none too happy about it.

1041
01:50:50,187 --> 01:50:53,357
Building parks is
the Parks Department's job.

1042
01:50:54,817 --> 01:50:57,986
We have our own proposals
for new parks.

1043
01:50:58,487 --> 01:51:01,156
Couldn't you reconsider?

1044
01:51:01,323 --> 01:51:03,826
This site is really terrible.

1045
01:51:03,992 --> 01:51:07,079
But building a park
isn't as simple

1046
01:51:07,287 --> 01:51:10,165
as you outline
in your proposal.

1047
01:51:10,332 --> 01:51:12,876
No, this proposal is just -

1048
01:51:13,043 --> 01:51:14,628
I understand.

1049
01:51:15,003 --> 01:51:16,672
Tell you what:

1050
01:51:17,089 --> 01:51:19,633
I'll study
your proposal thoroughly.

1051
01:51:23,887 --> 01:51:27,349
But you know,
in the end

1052
01:51:27,516 --> 01:51:30,853
even the parks chief caved in
to Watanabe's tenacity -

1053
01:51:31,019 --> 01:51:32,688
You've got a point there.

1054
01:51:32,855 --> 01:51:35,148
He really didn't know
what to do.

1055
01:52:40,589 --> 01:52:42,382
He really was something.

1056
01:52:42,549 --> 01:52:45,636
He would wait days
for the answer he wanted.

1057
01:52:45,802 --> 01:52:48,055
Our section chief
would sneak away

1058
01:52:48,180 --> 01:52:50,390
the second
he saw Watanabe's face.

1059
01:52:50,515 --> 01:52:51,683
That's right.

1060
01:53:03,070 --> 01:53:06,990
Section Chief!

1061
01:53:09,451 --> 01:53:12,788
He even pleaded
with underlings like me.

1062
01:53:12,955 --> 01:53:15,499
It was unbelievable!

1063
01:53:15,791 --> 01:53:18,085
Won't you please...?

1064
01:53:20,796 --> 01:53:22,798
Isn't there some way...?

1065
01:53:46,321 --> 01:53:48,240
Finally we couldn't
stand by any longer.

1066
01:53:49,741 --> 01:53:53,578
That's right.
We took pity on him too.

1067
01:53:53,745 --> 01:53:57,416
But you guys
in General Affairs

1068
01:53:58,000 --> 01:54:00,168
were the worst of all.

1069
01:54:00,293 --> 01:54:02,004
Wait a minute!

1070
01:54:02,129 --> 01:54:05,424
I went around with him,
so I know.

1071
01:54:05,590 --> 01:54:09,094
You made us beg for two weeks.
I won't forget that.

1072
01:54:09,302 --> 01:54:11,346
Sorry about that.

1073
01:54:12,097 --> 01:54:13,849
However...

1074
01:54:14,016 --> 01:54:16,560
remember
the most surprising thing?

1075
01:54:19,438 --> 01:54:22,274
- Oh, right.
- That was a shocker.

1076
01:54:56,683 --> 01:54:59,269
DEPUTY MAYOR'S OFFICE

1077
01:55:07,569 --> 01:55:10,572
But you know
what was even more surprising?

1078
01:55:10,739 --> 01:55:12,908
The incident
in the deputy mayor's office?

1079
01:55:13,241 --> 01:55:16,703
- I heard about that!
- That was something!

1080
01:55:19,372 --> 01:55:23,168
A section chief openly
defying the deputy mayor -

1081
01:55:23,335 --> 01:55:25,462
unprecedented
at City Hall!

1082
01:55:34,763 --> 01:55:38,058
Listen,
about that new park...

1083
01:55:38,850 --> 01:55:41,269
it's one thing
to show initiative,

1084
01:55:41,436 --> 01:55:45,941
but you don't want people
thinking you're a publicity hound.

1085
01:55:46,650 --> 01:55:50,278
And the city council's
got problems of its own.

1086
01:55:50,946 --> 01:55:55,242
I think it's best
just to let the proposal die.

1087
01:55:56,451 --> 01:56:00,038
Last night there was
a banquet at Nakagawa.

1088
01:56:00,288 --> 01:56:03,625
I tell you, geisha
these days are appalling.

1089
01:56:03,792 --> 01:56:06,378
One of them
spent the evening sulking

1090
01:56:06,545 --> 01:56:08,296
and didn't say a word.

1091
01:56:08,463 --> 01:56:12,467
The proprietress said she was
a student working part-time!

1092
01:56:14,010 --> 01:56:15,679
That's a good one!

1093
01:56:21,434 --> 01:56:24,479
Couldn't you please...

1094
01:56:26,565 --> 01:56:28,483
reconsider the proposal?

1095
01:56:33,989 --> 01:56:37,325
What did you just say?

1096
01:56:43,832 --> 01:56:45,333
I mean...

1097
01:56:46,626 --> 01:56:50,172
about that park...

1098
01:56:52,299 --> 01:56:55,260
won't you reconsider
your decision?

1099
01:57:39,054 --> 01:57:41,181
But the thing is,

1100
01:57:41,348 --> 01:57:43,767
when you look at the results,

1101
01:57:43,934 --> 01:57:45,977
was it worth taking it that far?

1102
01:57:46,144 --> 01:57:48,355
No, it was crazy!

1103
01:57:48,480 --> 01:57:52,859
Everyone at City Hall
is guarding his own turf.

1104
01:57:53,360 --> 01:57:57,906
But didn't the deputy mayor
end up reconsidering?

1105
01:57:58,031 --> 01:58:00,325
That's because
the councilman needled him.

1106
01:58:00,492 --> 01:58:02,244
It was just coincidence.

1107
01:58:02,410 --> 01:58:05,664
You're attributing everything
to Watanabe's “zeal.”

1108
01:58:05,830 --> 01:58:08,083
Don't be so sentimental!

1109
01:58:08,458 --> 01:58:09,793
Sentimental!

1110
01:58:10,252 --> 01:58:13,421
Is that so?
I don't see it that way.

1111
01:58:14,422 --> 01:58:17,717
If all of you can't
understand his dedication,

1112
01:58:17,884 --> 01:58:19,636
the world's
a dark place indeed.

1113
01:58:19,803 --> 01:58:21,596
It is a dark place.

1114
01:58:24,557 --> 01:58:28,520
From just looking
at Mr. Watanabe you could tell

1115
01:58:28,812 --> 01:58:32,774
that only his work
was keeping him alive.

1116
01:58:33,650 --> 01:58:36,111
Didn't it look
that way to you?

1117
01:58:36,945 --> 01:58:40,448
Sometimes it even
gave me chills.

1118
01:58:42,409 --> 01:58:44,411
When was it?

1119
01:58:44,869 --> 01:58:46,329
I was...

1120
01:59:31,166 --> 01:59:34,419
That reminds me
of when I'd see him

1121
01:59:34,586 --> 01:59:36,463
at the construction site.

1122
02:01:12,934 --> 02:01:14,936
That look in his eyes!

1123
02:01:15,103 --> 02:01:19,482
As if he were watching
his child or grandchild,

1124
02:01:19,732 --> 02:01:22,318
the apple of his eye.

1125
02:01:22,527 --> 02:01:24,279
Of course.

1126
02:01:24,571 --> 02:01:27,490
He nurtured that park
like you would a child.

1127
02:01:27,991 --> 02:01:29,701
So what are you saying?

1128
02:01:30,952 --> 02:01:34,622
Like I said before:

1129
02:01:35,290 --> 02:01:38,960
Whatever anyone says,
Mr. Watanabe built that park -

1130
02:01:39,127 --> 02:01:42,338
But if the councilman
and deputy mayor

1131
02:01:42,505 --> 02:01:44,591
hadn't been facing
reelection,

1132
02:01:44,757 --> 02:01:47,093
the plan wouldn't
have become a reality.

1133
02:01:47,260 --> 02:01:51,473
Watanabe had no role
in what was going on higher up.

1134
02:01:51,639 --> 02:01:54,142
That's not actually true.

1135
02:01:54,309 --> 02:01:55,727
Really?

1136
02:01:55,852 --> 02:01:59,022
The folks who wanted
that red-light district

1137
02:01:59,189 --> 02:02:03,485
definitely thought Watanabe was
blocking their way. Listen to this:

1138
02:02:20,418 --> 02:02:22,003
Section Chief.

1139
02:02:23,213 --> 02:02:26,508
Are you Section Chief
of Public Affairs?

1140
02:02:26,633 --> 02:02:28,176
Yes.

1141
02:02:32,388 --> 02:02:34,474
Just the man
I wanted to see.

1142
02:02:36,893 --> 02:02:40,396
Old man, you'd better
mind your own business.

1143
02:02:40,897 --> 02:02:44,067
Just what
are you trying to -

1144
02:02:44,234 --> 02:02:45,985
Don't play dumb!

1145
02:02:50,490 --> 02:02:52,325
No hard feelings.

1146
02:02:52,492 --> 02:02:55,078
Just shut up and back off.

1147
02:02:58,081 --> 02:02:59,666
Say something!

1148
02:02:59,916 --> 02:03:01,751
You value your life?

1149
02:03:21,813 --> 02:03:24,107
This is Watanabe.

1150
02:04:42,185 --> 02:04:44,270
I just don't get it.

1151
02:04:45,063 --> 02:04:48,858
Why would
someone like him -

1152
02:04:49,025 --> 02:04:51,194
I just don't...

1153
02:04:53,321 --> 02:04:55,031
get it.

1154
02:05:03,122 --> 02:05:07,543
No, I'm sure Mr. Watanabe knew
he had stomach cancer.

1155
02:05:07,710 --> 02:05:09,128
But -

1156
02:05:10,213 --> 02:05:14,550
I just suddenly
remembered something.

1157
02:05:28,773 --> 02:05:31,067
This is too much!

1158
02:05:31,234 --> 02:05:33,403
We've been at this
for two weeks.

1159
02:05:33,569 --> 02:05:35,571
We've explained it
to them 20 times.

1160
02:05:35,697 --> 02:05:38,491
They could at least tell us
if there are funds.

1161
02:05:38,616 --> 02:05:42,662
General Affairs is just being sadistic.
It's not like it's their personal money.

1162
02:05:42,829 --> 02:05:44,914
Why can't they be
more agreeable?

1163
02:05:45,081 --> 02:05:46,791
Well, you know -

1164
02:05:46,958 --> 02:05:49,335
Doesn't it make you furious

1165
02:05:49,585 --> 02:05:52,088
to be treated
with such contempt?

1166
02:05:53,756 --> 02:05:55,091
No.

1167
02:05:55,758 --> 02:05:58,428
I can't afford to hate people.

1168
02:06:01,097 --> 02:06:04,183
I haven't got
that kind of time.

1169
02:06:20,867 --> 02:06:21,784
You mean -

1170
02:06:21,951 --> 02:06:25,329
Wait!
Now that you mention it...

1171
02:06:26,038 --> 02:06:28,166
that reminds me...

1172
02:06:29,375 --> 02:06:31,794
of something.

1173
02:06:47,101 --> 02:06:49,812
How beautiful!

1174
02:06:58,070 --> 02:07:00,031
Truly beautiful.

1175
02:07:02,992 --> 02:07:05,244
A sunset.

1176
02:07:05,411 --> 02:07:08,581
I don't think I've really
looked at one in 30 years.

1177
02:07:16,172 --> 02:07:19,383
But I haven't got time
for this now.

1178
02:07:25,181 --> 02:07:27,183
He knew!

1179
02:07:27,350 --> 02:07:30,186
He knew he didn't have
long left to live -

1180
02:07:30,353 --> 02:07:33,523
It makes everything
clear as day.

1181
02:07:33,689 --> 02:07:35,399
There's no other explanation.

1182
02:07:35,566 --> 02:07:38,694
That explains
his extraordinary passion

1183
02:07:38,861 --> 02:07:41,239
and his strange behavior.

1184
02:07:41,697 --> 02:07:43,783
Now what he did
makes perfect sense!

1185
02:07:43,950 --> 02:07:46,536
Absolutely.
We'd have done the same.

1186
02:07:51,040 --> 02:07:54,460
But any of us
could die at any moment.

1187
02:08:07,557 --> 02:08:09,225
Listen...

1188
02:08:10,226 --> 02:08:13,896
Subsection Chief.

1189
02:08:17,233 --> 02:08:19,443
I mean you...

1190
02:08:19,610 --> 02:08:23,322
the new Section Chief
of Public Affairs.

1191
02:08:27,577 --> 02:08:30,788
Section Chief!
Are you listening?

1192
02:08:31,747 --> 02:08:35,084
I haven't been promoted yet.

1193
02:08:43,259 --> 02:08:44,760
See here!

1194
02:08:45,469 --> 02:08:48,055
Ono!
- Cut it out.

1195
02:08:48,180 --> 02:08:51,642
What did you say
a minute ago?

1196
02:08:52,435 --> 02:08:56,606
“We'd have done the same.”

1197
02:08:58,441 --> 02:09:00,359
Don't make me laugh.

1198
02:09:00,484 --> 02:09:02,278
Hey, that's enough now.

1199
02:09:02,445 --> 02:09:06,991
You think you could have done
what Mr. Watanabe did?

1200
02:09:08,451 --> 02:09:10,953
Don't make me laugh.

1201
02:09:11,621 --> 02:09:14,498
Listen, Ohara...

1202
02:09:15,708 --> 02:09:19,795
the thing is -

1203
02:09:20,463 --> 02:09:23,132
I may look dumb, but -

1204
02:09:23,299 --> 02:09:25,301
Don't you think...

1205
02:09:25,468 --> 02:09:29,680
Okay, I only went
to night school...

1206
02:09:29,889 --> 02:09:35,227
so I'll never make section chief!
- Watanabe was a great man!

1207
02:09:35,519 --> 02:09:38,397
No, great's not the right word.

1208
02:09:38,856 --> 02:09:41,233
- But you, Ono?
- What's the right word?

1209
02:09:41,400 --> 02:09:45,237
- Don't make me laugh.
- Compared to him -

1210
02:09:45,905 --> 02:09:49,909
Compared to Mr. Watanabe,
the rest of us -

1211
02:09:51,702 --> 02:09:54,372
We're worthless scum!

1212
02:09:56,374 --> 02:09:59,251
Including you!

1213
02:09:59,418 --> 02:10:01,504
That's going too far.

1214
02:10:04,674 --> 02:10:07,009
We may be scum...

1215
02:10:08,177 --> 02:10:12,932
but some of us start out
at City Hall as good people.

1216
02:10:13,265 --> 02:10:16,185
But the longer you stay -

1217
02:10:16,394 --> 02:10:18,187
I didn't used to be -

1218
02:10:20,856 --> 02:10:24,610
You're not supposed to do
anything at City Hall.

1219
02:10:24,777 --> 02:10:28,197
Doing anything
is considered radical!

1220
02:10:28,364 --> 02:10:31,909
We have to act like we're doing
something while doing nothing.

1221
02:10:32,076 --> 02:10:35,037
- That's right.
- In our department too!

1222
02:10:35,204 --> 02:10:37,206
The thing is,

1223
02:10:37,373 --> 02:10:41,419
just to get a garbage can
somewhere emptied out,

1224
02:10:41,752 --> 02:10:46,048
you need enough paperwork
to fill another garbage can.

1225
02:10:46,215 --> 02:10:48,217
That's exactly right!

1226
02:10:48,384 --> 02:10:50,219
Seal after seal after seal!

1227
02:10:50,344 --> 02:10:52,054
That's just an excuse.

1228
02:10:52,221 --> 02:10:54,598
We waste people's
valuable time.

1229
02:10:54,765 --> 02:10:58,728
The public is upset by bribes
and 40,000 cars and so on,

1230
02:10:58,894 --> 02:11:01,063
but those are just farts

1231
02:11:01,230 --> 02:11:04,233
next to this invisible,
colossal waste of time!

1232
02:11:07,737 --> 02:11:09,655
Listen here.

1233
02:11:09,780 --> 02:11:12,408
I often have
the same concerns.

1234
02:11:12,575 --> 02:11:16,912
But in that labyrinthine machine,
you can't do a thing!

1235
02:11:17,079 --> 02:11:20,249
All that meaningless busywork
and not a moment to think!

1236
02:11:20,416 --> 02:11:21,584
Idiots!

1237
02:11:30,926 --> 02:11:34,138
But the thing is,

1238
02:11:34,305 --> 02:11:35,765
Mr. Ohara,

1239
02:11:35,973 --> 02:11:38,100
even within a system

1240
02:11:38,267 --> 02:11:41,395
where you can't get
a thing done,

1241
02:11:41,812 --> 02:11:44,815
and battling
stomach cancer at that,

1242
02:11:44,982 --> 02:11:49,070
Mr. Watanabe managed
to accomplish so much.

1243
02:11:50,321 --> 02:11:53,616
That's it!
That's what I wanted to say!

1244
02:11:53,783 --> 02:11:56,285
That's why I get so angry!

1245
02:11:56,452 --> 02:11:58,537
That's what I'm saying!

1246
02:11:58,704 --> 02:12:01,457
Mr. Watanabe
expected no reward!

1247
02:12:01,582 --> 02:12:03,876
That's right!

1248
02:12:04,043 --> 02:12:07,713
When you imagine how he must
have felt, his life on the line -

1249
02:12:07,880 --> 02:12:10,299
Anyone who tries to take
the credit isn't human!

1250
02:12:10,466 --> 02:12:12,718
The deputy mayor.
Just say it!

1251
02:12:17,264 --> 02:12:19,683
That's going too far...

1252
02:12:27,900 --> 02:12:29,485
Listen.

1253
02:12:29,819 --> 02:12:35,032
How do you think he felt...

1254
02:12:36,492 --> 02:12:39,495
dying all alone in that park?

1255
02:12:40,037 --> 02:12:42,832
Just thinking about it...

1256
02:13:04,854 --> 02:13:09,942
A policeman brought this by.
He found it in the park.

1257
02:13:17,032 --> 02:13:20,619
And he'd like to light incense
for the deceased.

1258
02:13:39,096 --> 02:13:42,558
Thank you for taking the trouble.
Come in.

1259
02:14:41,450 --> 02:14:43,953
Won't you stay a bit
and have a drink?

1260
02:14:44,119 --> 02:14:46,455
We appreciate
your coming out of your way.

1261
02:14:48,123 --> 02:14:51,543
Please sit down.

1262
02:15:16,986 --> 02:15:18,821
To tell you the truth...

1263
02:15:24,243 --> 02:15:27,246
last night I was...

1264
02:15:28,038 --> 02:15:30,499
on patrol...

1265
02:15:32,167 --> 02:15:34,003
in the new park...

1266
02:15:37,840 --> 02:15:39,717
when I came across him.

1267
02:15:40,676 --> 02:15:43,345
It was 10:00 - no.

1268
02:15:45,848 --> 02:15:47,683
Nearly 11:00.

1269
02:15:48,350 --> 02:15:50,227
He was on the swing...

1270
02:15:50,686 --> 02:15:55,524
even though it was snowing.

1271
02:15:56,191 --> 02:15:58,861
I just assumed
he was some drunk.

1272
02:16:02,281 --> 02:16:05,034
I was derelict in my duty.

1273
02:16:10,456 --> 02:16:15,461
If I'd followed my first inclination
and taken him in,

1274
02:16:16,211 --> 02:16:18,464
this wouldn't have happened.

1275
02:16:20,382 --> 02:16:23,385
What I did was unforgivable.

1276
02:16:26,430 --> 02:16:29,725
But he seemed to be...

1277
02:16:31,393 --> 02:16:34,813
so perfectly happy.
I can't explain it.

1278
02:16:36,148 --> 02:16:40,486
He poured his whole heart
into that song of his...

1279
02:16:45,491 --> 02:16:47,910
with a haunting voice...

1280
02:16:50,412 --> 02:16:51,914
that filled...

1281
02:16:53,791 --> 02:16:58,170
the depths of my soul.

1282
02:17:02,424 --> 02:17:11,433
Life is brief

1283
02:17:13,811 --> 02:17:20,692
Fall in love, maidens

1284
02:17:24,279 --> 02:17:34,123
Before the crimson bloom

1285
02:17:35,541 --> 02:17:42,131
Fades from your lips

1286
02:17:46,218 --> 02:17:55,644
Before the tides of passion

1287
02:17:57,479 --> 02:18:03,986
Cool within you

1288
02:18:07,823 --> 02:18:17,332
For those of you

1289
02:18:19,084 --> 02:18:25,674
Who know no tomorrow

1290
02:18:48,030 --> 02:18:50,699
Listen, last night...

1291
02:18:51,825 --> 02:18:53,535
under the stairs,

1292
02:18:53,702 --> 02:18:56,246
I found a pouch
with my name on it.

1293
02:18:56,622 --> 02:18:59,500
Inside were
Dad's bankbook and seal,

1294
02:18:59,625 --> 02:19:04,129
along with forms for claiming
his retirement bonus.

1295
02:19:04,588 --> 02:19:07,382
So he left it
before he went to the park?

1296
02:19:07,549 --> 02:19:10,886
It was so cruel of him...

1297
02:19:11,553 --> 02:19:14,932
not to tell us he had cancer.
If I'd known sooner -

1298
02:19:15,974 --> 02:19:19,645
Hey, why didn't
his girlfriend show up?

1299
02:19:20,562 --> 02:19:23,524
I wonder if she really was
his girlfriend after all.

1300
02:19:28,070 --> 02:19:29,571
I'll do my best!

1301
02:19:29,738 --> 02:19:32,074
I swear it!
- We must carry on his work!

1302
02:19:32,241 --> 02:19:35,744
We can't let
his death be in vain!

1303
02:19:36,912 --> 02:19:40,290
I'll be like a man reborn!

1304
02:19:47,798 --> 02:19:50,842
We'll sacrifice ourselves
in the service of the people!

1305
02:19:54,596 --> 02:19:57,015
Never forget this feeling!

1306
02:19:57,182 --> 02:19:59,601
I'm going to do my best!

1307
02:20:04,773 --> 02:20:07,776
PUBLIC AFFAIRS
SECTION CHIEF

1308
02:20:16,285 --> 02:20:17,703
Sir...

1309
02:20:17,869 --> 02:20:21,957
the sewage main break in Kisaki
is overflowing into Takao.

1310
02:20:22,124 --> 02:20:23,875
Public Works.

1311
02:20:29,131 --> 02:20:31,800
Please go to Public Works.

1312
02:20:31,967 --> 02:20:33,677
Desk 8.

1313
02:21:49,211 --> 02:21:51,713
Kenbo! Yoko!

1314
02:21:54,716 --> 02:21:56,385
Come in for dinner!

1315
02:22:51,773 --> 02:22:54,818
THE END
